{
  "title": "Cluster Measurements (UMAP/HDBSCAN)",
  "cn": {
    "func": "对 MultiROI 的逐标签测量表进行无监督聚类。选择一个带有测量标量槽（scalar slot）的 MultiROI，勾选要参与聚类的测量项作为特征，选择降维方法（UMAP/t-SNE/PCA，降到二维）与聚类算法（KMeans 指定簇数 / HDBSCAN 指定最小簇大小），并可选择是否做标准化（StandardScaler）。插件将每个标签的聚类编号写回为一个新的 MultiROI 标量槽，并生成一张二维散点图 PNG，便于按聚类结果着色显示与分组分析。",
    "scenarios": "适用于材料与生命科学中的颗粒/孔隙/晶粒/细胞分群——依据体积、表面积、形状因子、球度、取向等逐标签测量，将成千上万个标签自动归类为若干形态或成分相近的群体，用于分类统计、异常检测与结构表征。",
    "requirements": "首次使用点击 Setup Environment（使用 Dragonfly 自带 Python 构建本地计算环境，安装 scikit-learn、umap-learn、hdbscan、matplotlib，需联网一次）。无需 GPU。在 Dragonfly 2027.1（Python 3.14）上不能用 Dragonfly 自带的 Python 创建该环境：请安装 python.org 的 Python（3.12、3.13 或 3.14）并填入面板的 Base Python；Dragonfly 2025.1 无需另装 Python。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Unsupervised clustering of a MultiROI's per-label measurement table. Pick a MultiROI that carries measurement scalar slots, tick which measurements to use as features, choose a dimensionality reducer (UMAP/t-SNE/PCA to 2D) and a clusterer (KMeans with a fixed number of clusters, or HDBSCAN with a minimum cluster size), and optionally standardize the features (StandardScaler). The plugin writes each label's cluster id back as a NEW MultiROI scalar slot and saves a 2D scatter PNG, so you can color the MultiROI by cluster and analyze the groups.",
    "scenarios": "Grouping particles, pores, grains, or cells in materials and life science - using per-label measurements such as volume, surface area, shape factor, sphericity, or orientation to automatically sort thousands of labels into a handful of morphologically or compositionally similar groups for classification, outlier detection, and structural characterization.",
    "requirements": "First use: click Setup Environment (builds a local compute environment from Dragonfly's own Python and installs scikit-learn, umap-learn, hdbscan, matplotlib; needs internet once). No GPU required. On Dragonfly 2027.1 (Python 3.14) the bundled Python cannot build this environment: install a python.org Python (3.12, 3.13 or 3.14) and enter it in the panel's Base Python field; Dragonfly 2025.1 needs no separate Python."
  }
}

{
  "title": "Photo2Mesh: COLMAP",
  "cn": {
    "func": "利用经典的 COLMAP 摄影测量流程（运动恢复结构 + 稠密重建 + 网格化），从围绕物体拍摄的一组普通照片重建其三维表面网格。您只需指定照片来源——Dragonfly 中当前的图像堆栈或一个照片文件夹——选择质量等级和网格化方法，点击\"重建\"即可；生成的表面网格会直接发布到 Dragonfly 场景中。注意重建结果的尺度和方向是任意的，如需真实尺寸请在导入后重新缩放。",
    "scenarios": "适用于在没有 CT 或激光扫描仪的情况下获得实物的三维表面数字模型——例如通过一组照片记录地质或生物标本、化石、工业零件、铸件或考古样品，或生成用于与 CT 数据对比的参考表面。最适合拍摄重叠充分、不透明且纹理丰富的物体。",
    "requirements": "首次使用通过设置按钮下载独立的 COLMAP 程序包（需联网）；稠密重建需要 NVIDIA CUDA 显卡。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Reconstructs a 3D surface mesh of an object from a series of ordinary photos taken around it, using the classical COLMAP photogrammetry pipeline (structure-from-motion plus dense reconstruction and meshing). You point it at your photos — either the active image stack in Dragonfly or a folder of photo files — choose a quality level and meshing method, and click Reconstruct; the finished surface mesh is published directly into the Dragonfly scene. Note that the reconstruction has arbitrary scale and orientation, so rescale the mesh after import if real-world dimensions matter.",
    "scenarios": "Useful whenever you want a digital 3D surface model of a physical object without a CT or laser scanner — e.g. documenting geological or biological specimens, fossils, industrial parts, casts, or archaeological samples from a set of photos, or creating reference surfaces to compare against CT data. Works best on opaque, well-textured objects photographed with good overlap.",
    "requirements": "First use downloads a self-contained COLMAP package via the setup button (needs internet); dense reconstruction requires an NVIDIA CUDA GPU."
  }
}

{
  "title": "Color Image Processing",
  "cn": {
    "func": "功能：处理已导入 Dragonfly 并被拆分为红/绿/蓝三个通道的彩色（RGB）图像，共七个标签页，顶部为所有标签页共用：颜色来源（三个通道，或**一个含三分量的通道**）、数值范围、切面、切片与时间步。**通道自动识别**：第一个下拉框自动选中第一个名称以「Red」开头的图像通道，第二、第三个下拉框再从名称与之相同的通道中选出对应的绿色和蓝色通道——因此会话中同时存在两幅彩色图像时，不会把「Green - Sample 2」和「Red - Sample 1」配到一起；找不到同名通道时会退而选用第一个绿色/蓝色通道，并明确提示三者可能不属于同一幅图像。（1）**彩色显示**：Dragonfly 每次只能通过查找表显示一个通道，无法显示 RGB 彩色图像，本标签页正是补上这一缺口——真彩色显示、XY/XZ/YZ 三个切面、缩放，鼠标悬停即读出该像素的 RGB、HSV 与 CIE-L*a*b*。（2）**色彩调整**：白平衡（灰度世界、白块分位数、或点选一个中性灰像素）、各通道黑场/白场色阶（含 1 %/99 % 自动按钮）、伽马、亮度、对比度、饱和度、色相，按**固定且写明的顺序**执行，并可选择让这些调整对其它标签页和导出同时生效。（3）**颜色阈值**：即 ImageJ 的 Color Threshold，支持 RGB / HSV / CIE-L*a*b* / YCbCr 四个颜色空间，最小值大于最大值即表示选取「环绕」区间（跨红色的色相区间就是这样写的），可拾取颜色加容差、开/闭运算、最小面积、填充孔洞，并实时给出选中比例与面积。因为按颜色阈值分割**本身就是图像分割**，其结果可发布为 **MultiROI**——整个选区作为一个标签，或每个连通区域各占一个标签（跨整叠切片统一编号，而不是每张切片重新开始）——也仍可发布为 ROI。（4）**颜色分离**：拆分为颜色空间分量，或用 Ruifrok–Johnston 光密度反卷积按颜色向量解混，内置八种染色预设，也可直接从图像上拾取颜色向量；**负值不会被隐藏**，因为负值正是「这组向量不适合这幅图像」的信号。（5）**晶粒（IPF）**：按颜色把取向着色的 EBSD 图分割成晶粒（相邻像素 CIE76 色差超过容差即为晶界），给出等效直径分布与 D10/D50/D90，二维图另给 ASTM E112 面积法晶粒度 G，可保存逐晶粒 CSV，并发布为按各晶粒自身平均颜色着色的 MultiROI。（6）**颜色聚类**：找出图像中的颜色**类别**——某种颜色的所有体素，无论位于何处，都归入同一类别——可自动确定数量（按 ΔE 容差）或指定个数 k；结果以表格列出，可合并两个、拆分一个、增加或减少一个，确认后发布为 MultiROI。本标签页取代了已停用的 Color Image to MultiROI 插件。它与「晶粒」标签页的问题恰好相反：同一颜色的两个分离区域，在这里是**一个**类别，在那里是**两个**晶粒。（7）**元素分布图（EDS）**：以你列出或点选的参考颜色（另有「从当前切片推荐」按钮）测量元素着色合成图——「分类」把每个像素归入最近的参考颜色，给出互不重叠的面积分数；「解混」用最小二乘求解最多三种参考颜色的含量，并给出共定位统计（交集、Jaccard 以及**两个方向**的条件比例）。底部的导出栏对七个标签页一视同仁：把结果导出为 PNG / TIFF（可合并为一个多页文件）/ BMP / JPEG，并把像素尺寸写入文件；或发布回 Dragonfly——彩色结果发布为红/绿/蓝三个通道，掩膜发布为 ROI，**分割结果发布为 MultiROI**，其每个标签都带有自己的颜色与**名称**（元素分布图会直接以 Fe / O / Si 命名）。所有做分割的标签页都把 MultiROI 放在列表的**第一项**，因此点「发布」默认发布的就是分割结果；而分割结果的**彩色渲染图**（以平均颜色显示的晶粒、以参考颜色显示的元素、选区着色图）只能导出为图像文件，故意不允许发布——发布它们只会得到三个 RGB 通道，而其中的颜色是本插件画上去的，并非分割结果本身。不按「发布」就不会创建或修改任何会话对象。",
    "scenarios": "适用场景：查看 Dragonfly 无法真彩色显示的 RGB 数据（岩相薄片、组织切片、彩色相机图像、SEM 伪彩合成图）；对彩色图像做白平衡与色阶校正后再交给后续分析；按颜色分割出某一相／某一染色区域并发布为 ROI；把 H&E、H-DAB 等染色切片分离成各染色成分；把取向着色（IPF）的 EBSD 截图按颜色分成晶粒并统计晶粒尺寸；把 EDS 元素分布合成图按元素参考颜色定量，得到各元素面积分数与两两共定位；把处理后的彩色图像导出为 PNG/TIFF 交给论文、报告或其它软件；把校正后的彩色图像作为三个通道重新发布回 Dragonfly 继续使用。",
    "requirements": "环境需求：无需安装、联网、GPU、管理员权限或虚拟环境——进程内 PyQt6 加上 Dragonfly 自带的 numpy、scipy 与 Pillow。需要会话中有同一幅彩色图像的三个已发布通道，且三者尺寸必须相同（不同则明确拒绝）。整数通道按其数据类型的完整范围映射到 0–1，浮点通道按实测范围，也可自定义范围。像素尺寸不可读时不会给出面积或 ASTM 晶粒度，而是明确说明；单张切片给出面积，真正的三维体给出体积。**发布必须选择「全部切片」**：新对象沿用源数据的原点，只发布部分切片会把结果画在错误的位置，此时请改为导出文件。晶粒标签页是基于**颜色**的分割，不解码取向、不计算取向差；真正的取向数据请使用可读取 .ang / .ctf 的 EBSD 插件。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "What it does: works on a colour (RGB) image that Dragonfly has split into Red / Green / Blue channels, in seven tabs that share one source selector at the top: the colour source (three channels, or ONE channel carrying three components), the value range, the viewing plane, the slice and the time step. **Channel auto-detection**: the first box takes the first image channel whose title starts with \"Red\", and the second and third then take the Green and Blue channels whose names match THAT image - so with two colour images in the session, \"Green - Sample 2\" is never paired with \"Red - Sample 1\"; when no name matches it falls back to the first green/blue and says plainly that the three may not belong together. (1) **Color view**: Dragonfly renders one channel at a time through a lookup table and cannot show an RGB composite at all, which is the gap this tab fills - true colour, XY/XZ/YZ planes, zoom, and a hover readout giving that pixel's RGB, HSV and CIE-L*a*b*. (2) **Adjust**: white balance (gray world, white-patch percentile, or from a neutral pixel you click), per-channel black/white levels with an auto 1 %/99 % button, gamma, brightness, contrast, saturation and hue, applied in a FIXED, documented order, optionally feeding every other tab and the export. (3) **Color threshold**: ImageJ's Color Threshold in RGB / HSV / CIE-L*a*b* / YCbCr, where a band whose minimum is above its maximum selects the WRAPPED band (that is how a hue band across red is written), with pick-a-colour-plus-tolerance, opening/closing, a minimum size, hole filling and live selected-percentage and area. Because thresholding by colour IS a segmentation, its result publishes as a **MultiROI** - the selection as one label, or the connected pieces as one label each, labelled across the whole stack rather than restarted on every slice - and as an ROI if you prefer one. (4) **Separate**: split into colour-space components, or unmix by colour vectors with Ruifrok-Johnston optical-density deconvolution - eight stain presets plus vectors picked from the image itself. Negative amounts are NOT hidden: they are the signal that the vectors do not describe this image. (5) **Grains (IPF)**: segments an orientation-coloured EBSD map into grains by colour (neighbouring pixels further apart than a CIE76 tolerance are a boundary), reports the equivalent-diameter distribution with D10/D50/D90 and, for a 2-D map, the ASTM E112 planimetric grain size number G, saves a per-grain CSV and publishes a MultiROI coloured by each grain's own mean colour. (6) **Color clusters**: finds the colour CLASSES of the image - every voxel of a colour becomes that class wherever it sits - either automatically (as many colours as the image has, by a ΔE tolerance) or as a fixed number k, then lets you review them in a table and merge two / split one / add / remove one before publishing them as a MultiROI. It replaces the retired Color Image to MultiROI plugin. This is the opposite question from the Grains tab: two separate regions of one colour are ONE class there and TWO grains here. (7) **Element maps (EDS)**: measures an element-coloured composite against reference colours you list or click (with a \"suggest from this slice\" button) - CLASSIFY puts every pixel in its nearest reference for disjoint area fractions, UNMIX solves least-squares for up to three references and reports co-localization (intersection, Jaccard and BOTH conditional fractions). One export footer serves all seven tabs: write the result as PNG / TIFF (optionally one multi-page file) / BMP / JPEG with the pixel size inside the file, or publish it back into Dragonfly - a colour result as three Red/Green/Blue channels, a mask as an ROI, a segmentation as a MultiROI whose labels carry their own colours AND names (an element map arrives as Fe / O / Si). Every tab that segments offers its MultiROI FIRST, so Publish defaults to the segmentation; the coloured RENDERINGS of a segmentation (grains in their mean colour, elements in their reference colours, a tinted selection) can be exported as image files but deliberately cannot be published, because publishing them would add three RGB channels of colour this plugin painted on rather than the segmentation itself. Nothing in the session is created or modified until you press Publish.",
    "scenarios": "Typical scenarios: looking at RGB data Dragonfly cannot display in colour (petrographic thin sections, histology slides, colour-camera images, SEM false-colour composites); white-balancing and levelling a colour image before further analysis; thresholding one phase or one stain by colour and publishing it as an ROI; separating an H&E or H-DAB section into its stains; splitting a screenshot of an orientation-coloured (IPF) EBSD map into grains and measuring the size distribution; quantifying an EDS element composite against per-element reference colours to get area fractions and pairwise co-localization; exporting the processed colour image as PNG/TIFF for a paper, a report or another program; publishing the corrected colour image back into Dragonfly as three channels.",
    "requirements": "Requirements: no install, no internet, no GPU, no admin rights, no venv - in-process PyQt6 plus the numpy, scipy and Pillow Dragonfly already ships. Needs three published channels of the SAME colour image; three channels of different sizes are refused rather than mixed. Integer channels are mapped to 0-1 through their data type's full range, float channels through a measured range, and a custom range is available. When the pixel size cannot be read, no area and no ASTM grain size are given and the panel says so; a single slice gets an AREA, a real volume a VOLUME. **Publishing requires \"All slices\"**: the new object inherits the source's origin, so a partial stack would be drawn in the wrong place - export a sub-range to files instead. The Grains tab is a COLOUR segmentation: it decodes no orientation and computes no misorientation, and for real orientation data the EBSD plugins that read .ang / .ctf files are the right tool."
  }
}

{
  "title_en": "Color Image Processing",
  "title_cn": "Color Image Processing（彩色图像处理）",
  "menu_en": "Color Image Processing...",
  "version": "1.0",
  "date": "2026-09-01",
  "en": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Dragonfly renders **one channel at a time through a lookup table** and cannot display an RGB composite. A colour image imported into Dragonfly therefore arrives as three grayscale channels — `Red - ...`, `Green - ...`, `Blue - ...` — that can only be looked at separately. This plugin puts them back together and does the colour work on top of them."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The panel has one **source selector** at the top (the three channels, the value range, the viewing plane, the slice and the time step), **six tabs** that all read that same source, and one **export/publish footer** that serves every tab."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**Nothing in the session is created or modified until you press Publish.** Everything else reads the three channels and writes files where you point it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "It runs in Dragonfly's own process on the numpy, scipy and Pillow Dragonfly already ships: no install, no internet, no GPU, no virtual environment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The source: three channels of one image",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The first box is filled with the first image channel whose title **starts with \"Red\"**. The second and third are then filled with the Green and Blue channels whose names match that same image, so with two colour images open in the session `Green - Sample 2` is never paired with `Red - Sample 1`."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "When no matching name exists, the first Green / Blue channel is used instead and the panel says the three **may belong to another image**."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "A title that merely contains the letters (`Reduction`) is not a red channel: whole words are matched, at the start of the title first, then at the end."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**Refresh** re-reads the session after you publish something new; **Auto-detect** runs the matching again."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Value range** decides how channel values become colour. *Auto* uses the data type's full range (0–255 for 8-bit, 0–65535 for 16-bit); float channels have no natural range and are measured instead. *Measured* uses each channel's own minimum and maximum. *Custom* applies one range to all three."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Plane** chooses XY, XZ or YZ; the slider walks the remaining axis, and every tab — and the export — follows it. **Time step** picks the frame of a 4-D channel."
        },
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "The three channels must have the same size. Three channels of different sizes are refused with their sizes printed, rather than being stretched to match."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Tab 1 — Color view",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The RGB composite, at last. Use the plane, slice and **Zoom** controls to navigate; **hover** over a pixel to read its position, RGB, HSV and CIE-L*a*b* values. The R / G / B tick boxes switch a channel off (useful for seeing which channel carries a feature), and one more shows the luminance instead of the colour."
        },
        {
          "kind": "image",
          "file": "manual_images/en/01_color_view.png",
          "caption": "The Color view tab. Dragonfly renders one channel at a time through a LUT and cannot draw an RGB composite at all — this tab is that composite."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "When the Adjust tab is switched on, both the picture and the hover readout show the **adjusted** values, and the status line says so."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Exportable layers: the colour image, and its luminance as a grayscale image."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Tab 2 — Adjust",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
