  "version": "1.0",
  "date": "2026-07-26",
  "cn": [
    {"heading": "简介", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "**Create Synthetic CAD Models (STEP)** 从多种预设的**参数化形状/设计**中生成真实的 CAD 实体，并保存为 **STEP** 文件；在 Dragonfly 2027.1 及以上版本中还可一键把 STEP 作为网格（Mesh）加载到当前场景。"},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "几何由开源库 **cadquery**（OpenCASCADE / `cadquery-ocp`，Apache-2.0）生成，运行在插件专用的虚拟环境（venv）子进程中，不会影响 Dragonfly 自身的 Python。所有尺寸单位为**毫米（mm）**。"},
      {"kind": "note", "text": "本功能由第三方（Prototype Labs / Prototype Apps）提供，非 Dragonfly 官方开发或支持，使用风险自负。"}
    ]},
    {"heading": "支持的形状", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "形状按三组组织，可在下拉框中选择："},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**基本体（Primitives）**：长方体、圆柱、球、圆锥/圆台、圆环、四棱锥、楔形。"},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**机械零件（Mechanical Parts）**：六角螺栓、六角螺母、垫圈、正齿轮、L 形支架、螺栓圆法兰、管件。"},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**测试标定件（Test Artifacts）**：孔阵列板（CAD 孔隙率假体）、阶梯规、球-柱标定件（CT）、多孔立方体、圆角/倒角块。"},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "选择形状后，右侧会自动列出该形状的参数（如长/宽/高、半径、齿数、孔径等），可逐一调整。"}
    ]},
    {"heading": "使用步骤", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "steps", "steps": [
        "从下拉框选择一个形状/设计，并设置其参数。",
        "在“Output”中确认或用“Browse…”选择 STEP 保存文件夹。",
        "**首次使用**：点击一次“⚙ 设置环境”，构建 cadquery 虚拟环境（需联网，约数百 MB）。",
        "点击“**创建 CAD 模型并保存 STEP 文件**”，稍候即可在所选文件夹得到 `<形状>_<时间戳>.step`。",
        "（可选）点击“**加载到 Dragonfly**”，把刚生成的 STEP 作为网格导入当前场景。"
      ]}
    ]},
    {"heading": "环境需求", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "首次点击“设置环境”会用 Dragonfly 自带 Python 在 `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\CreateSyntheticCAD\\venv`（**较短路径**，以规避 Windows 路径长度限制）构建 venv，并安装 `cadquery` + `numpy`。之后生成不再需要联网，也无需 GPU。"},
      {"kind": "note", "text": "“加载到 Dragonfly”仅在 **Dragonfly 2027.1 及以上**版本可用（更早版本没有 STEP 导入）。在旧版本中该按钮会被禁用并给出提示；STEP 文件仍可正常生成、供其它软件使用。"}
    ]},
    {"heading": "提示", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "STEP 是 B-rep（精确边界表示）实体；加载到 Dragonfly 时会经 OpenCASCADE 三角化为网格（`MeshHelper.importSTEPFile`）。"},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "生成的 STEP 也可直接用于网格划分、有限元/CFD 仿真、逆向、或作为算法测试的标准件。"}
    ]}
  ],
  "en": [
    {"heading": "Introduction", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "**Create Synthetic CAD Models (STEP)** builds real CAD solids from a menu of preset **parametric shapes/designs** and writes them as **STEP** files. On Dragonfly 2027.1+, it can also load a STEP into the current scene as a Mesh with one click."},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "Geometry is built with the open-source **cadquery** library (OpenCASCADE / `cadquery-ocp`, Apache-2.0), running in the plugin's dedicated venv as a subprocess so it never touches Dragonfly's own Python. All dimensions are in **millimetres (mm)**."},
      {"kind": "note", "text": "This feature is provided by a third party (Prototype Labs / Apps), not officially developed or supported by Dragonfly. Use at your own risk."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Available shapes", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "Shapes are organised into three groups in the dropdown:"},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Primitives**: box, cylinder, sphere, cone/frustum, torus, square pyramid, wedge."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Mechanical Parts**: hex bolt, hex nut, washer, spur gear, L-bracket, bolt-circle flange, pipe."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Test Artifacts**: hole-array plate (CAD porosity phantom), step gauge, sphere-on-stem (CT), perforated cube, fillet/chamfer block."},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "Selecting a shape lists its parameters (length/width/height, radius, tooth count, hole diameter, …) for you to edit."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "How to use", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "steps", "steps": [
        "Pick a shape/design from the dropdown and set its parameters.",
        "Confirm the STEP output folder under 'Output' (or 'Browse…').",
        "First time only: click '⚙ Setup Environment' once to build the cadquery venv (needs internet, a few hundred MB).",
        "Click '**Create CAD Model and Save STEP File**' — a `<shape>_<timestamp>.step` appears in the chosen folder.",
        "(Optional) Click '**Load to Dragonfly**' to import the STEP you just created as a Mesh."
      ]}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Requirements", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "The first 'Setup Environment' builds a venv at `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\CreateSyntheticCAD\\venv` (a **short** path, to keep OpenCASCADE DLLs under the Windows MAX_PATH limit) from Dragonfly's own Python and installs `cadquery` + `numpy`. After that, generation needs no internet and no GPU."},
      {"kind": "note", "text": "'Load to Dragonfly' works only on **Dragonfly 2027.1+** (older versions have no STEP import). On older versions the button is disabled with a tooltip; the STEP file is still produced for use in other software."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Tips", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "STEP is a B-rep (exact boundary) solid; loading into Dragonfly triangulates it into a Mesh via OpenCASCADE (`MeshHelper.importSTEPFile`)."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "The STEP files are also ready for downstream meshing, FE/CFD simulation, reverse engineering, or as standard parts for algorithm testing."}
    ]}
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Create Synthetic Images",
  "cn": {
    "func": "生成多种类型的二维/三维合成图像数据并作为 Channel 导入。内置 26 种生成器，分六大类：形状与体模（Shepp-Logan、球/立方体/圆柱、同心壳）、噪声（高斯、均匀、椒盐、分形 fBm）、多孔与颗粒（相关斑点、多种球体堆积、纤维网络）、微结构（Voronoi 晶界与晶粒、Sierpinski 泡沫、Cantor 尘）、细胞与斑点，以及测试图案（渐变、棋盘、正弦光栅、Siemens 星、同心环）。选择生成器与参数、二维/三维尺寸后点击生成即可（面板显示中切片预览）。",
    "scenarios": "适用于算法测试与教学演示、为分割/测量/重建流程制作已知真值（ground truth）数据，以及在没有真实数据时快速验证工作流。",
    "requirements": "无需搭建环境、无需下载：所需依赖（numpy/scipy/scikit-image/porespy）均已随 Dragonfly 附带，生成过程以 Dragonfly 自带 Python 的子进程运行，点开即用。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Generates many kinds of synthetic 2D/3D image data and imports them as Channels. 26 generators in six families: shapes & phantoms (Shepp-Logan, sphere/cube/cylinder, concentric shells), noise (Gaussian, uniform, salt & pepper, fractal fBm), porous media & particles (correlated blobs, various sphere packings, fiber networks), microstructure (Voronoi edges/grains, Sierpinski foam, Cantor dust), cells & blobs, and test patterns (gradient, checkerboard, sinusoid, Siemens star, concentric rings). Pick a generator and its parameters, choose 2D or 3D and the output size, and click Generate (a mid-slice preview shows in the panel).",
    "scenarios": "Algorithm testing and teaching demos, producing known ground truth for segmentation / measurement / reconstruction pipelines, and quickly validating a workflow when no real data is at hand.",
    "requirements": "No setup, no download: every dependency (numpy/scipy/scikit-image/porespy) already ships with Dragonfly, and generation runs as a subprocess of Dragonfly's own Python - just click and go."
  }
}

{
  "title": "Deep Denoising (CSBDeep / Noise2Void)…",
  "cn": {
    "func": "使用 TensorFlow 2 对图像进行内容感知修复（去噪）。提供两种模式：CARE（监督式，需要成对的\"含噪\"与\"干净\"图像 Channel）和 Noise2Void（自监督，只需含噪图像、无需干净参考）。在 Train（训练）标签页添加一个或多个训练样本并训练模型；在 Apply（应用）标签页选择已保存的模型和某个 Channel 进行预测，去噪后的结果会作为一个新的 Channel 发布回场景，可直接用于测量与可视化。\n\n「Setup」页的**一键执行全部步骤（按当前设置）**会无人值守地跑完流程：环境安装 → 添加训练用例 → 训练 → 去噪并发布，每一步都使用你按下按钮那一刻各自页签上显示的设置。**环境安装是复选框且默认关闭**（要建虚拟环境并安装 TensorFlow 2、csbdeep 与 n2v：几个 GB，每台机器装一次，离线无法完成）。**训练也是复选框，但默认开启**——这一点与同类的 nnU-Net 插件相反，因为本插件出厂默认是 20 个 epoch × 100 步，只需几分钟，而不是 nnU-Net 固定的 1000 个 epoch；如果你已经有训练好的模型，取消勾选即可直接去噪。最后一步会发布一个新的 Channel；如果这一步始终没能启动，运行会如实报告为**已停止**并给出原因，而不是报告「已完成」。所有页签始终可以打开和修改参数，以便在运行**之前**逐页检查；某一步所需的输入尚不存在时，会在**它自己的按钮**上给出提示，说明缺的是哪一步，而不是把整个页签锁住不让进入。",
    "scenarios": "适用于任何需要在测量或分割之前降低图像噪声的场景：低剂量或快速采集的 CT、含噪的显微镜与荧光体数据、材料科学与工业扫描中的斑点噪声等。当没有干净的参考图像时，使用 Noise2Void 仅凭含噪数据即可训练去噪模型；当拥有配对的高质量参考图像时，使用 CARE 可获得更强的修复效果。",
    "requirements": "首次使用请点击\"Setup Environment\"（使用 Dragonfly 自带 Python 构建运行环境，需从网络下载 TensorFlow 2 + csbdeep + n2v，共数 GB）；需要联网，并建议配备 NVIDIA GPU（以获得可用的训练/预测速度）。n2v 锁定 TensorFlow 2（n2v 0.3.x）。在 Dragonfly 2027.1（Python 3.14）上不能用 Dragonfly 自带的 Python 创建该环境：请安装 python.org 的 Python（3.12、3.13 或 3.14）并填入面板的 Base Python；Dragonfly 2025.1 无需另装 Python。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Performs content-aware image restoration (denoising) with TensorFlow 2. Two modes are provided: CARE (supervised, needs paired 'noisy' and 'clean' image Channels) and Noise2Void (self-supervised, needs only the noisy image — no clean reference). In the Train tab you add one or more training cases and train a model; in the Apply tab you pick a saved model and a Channel to predict on, and the denoised result is published back into the scene as a new Channel, ready for measurement and visualization.\n\n**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the Setup tab runs the workflow unattended - set up the environment, add a training case, train, denoise and publish - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. Setting up the environment is a tick box, OFF by default (a venv plus TensorFlow 2, csbdeep and n2v: several GB, once per machine, impossible offline). Training is a tick box, ON by default - unlike the sibling nnU-Net plugin, because the shipped defaults are 20 epochs of 100 steps, which is minutes rather than nnU-Net's fixed thousand; untick it to denoise with a model you already have. The last step publishes a new Channel, and a run in which it never started reports itself as stopped with the reason rather than as finished. Every tab stays open at all times so the parameters can be read and changed BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses at its own button and says which step is missing, instead of the tab being locked shut.",
    "scenarios": "Useful whenever image noise should be reduced before measurement or segmentation: low-dose or fast-acquisition CT, noisy microscopy and fluorescence volumes, speckle in materials-science and industrial scans. When no clean reference exists, Noise2Void trains a denoiser from the noisy data alone; when paired high-quality references are available, CARE gives stronger restoration.",
    "requirements": "First use: click Setup Environment (builds a Python environment with Dragonfly's own Python and downloads TensorFlow 2 + csbdeep + n2v, several GB); needs internet and, for practical training/prediction speed, an NVIDIA GPU. n2v pins TensorFlow 2 (n2v 0.3.x). On Dragonfly 2027.1 (Python 3.14) the bundled Python cannot build this environment: install a python.org Python (3.12, 3.13 or 3.14) and enter it in the panel's Base Python field; Dragonfly 2025.1 needs no separate Python."
  }
}

{
  "title": "Learning about CT Reconstruction (Hands-on Workshop)",
  "cn": {
    "func": "功能：一个讲解 CT（计算机断层成像）原理与图像重建原理的上手练习项目。三个标签页 + 证书页：物体/体模（要“扫描”的衰减分布——一键生成合成体模、体素化 STEP/STL 网格、直接调用“创建合成 CAD 模型（STEP）”插件自己设计，或选用一个 Channel）→ 前向投影（设置投影数/角度范围/噪声，调用 Radon 变换生成投影图像和正弦图 sinogram 并发布）→ 重建（滤波反投影 FBP 选不同滤波器，或迭代 SART，与体模真值对比 RMSE）。全部动手环节在进程内用 numpy/scikit-image 运行，无需安装、无需 GPU、无需虚拟环境。用少角度 vs 多角度、ramp vs none 滤波器直观演示欠采样条纹等伪影。\n\n第一个页签上的**一键执行全部步骤（按当前设置）**会无人值守地跑完整个教学循环：获取被扫描物体 → 正向投影 → 发布中间层正弦图 → 重建，每一步都使用你按下按钮那一刻各自页签上显示的设置。它**不取代**手动操作：每个分步按钮和每篇讲解文档都原样保留。它是为「第二遍」准备的——改一个参数后把整个循环重跑一次——以及用于演示完整流程。两个复选框**默认都关闭**：「生成新的体模」并不是跳过第一步，而是决定这一步究竟是**新造一个物体**还是**扫描当前已选中的物体**，这样学习者自己载入的 CAD 网格就不会被替换掉；「发布正弦图」代价很小，但会多出一个重建并不需要的教学用 Channel。网格体素化、打开 CAD 插件、保存证书 PDF 都不在流程内：它们各自需要文件对话框或另一个插件的窗口。所有页签始终可以打开和修改参数，以便在运行**之前**逐页检查；某一步所需的输入尚不存在时，会在**它自己的按钮**上给出提示，说明缺的是哪一步，而不是把整个页签锁住不让进入。",
    "scenarios": "适用场景：理解 CT 成像原理（X 射线衰减 Beer–Lambert、投影/正弦图、平行/扇/锥束几何）与重建原理（反投影为何必须滤波、FBP 滤波器权衡、迭代重建 SART、少角度/有限角/射束硬化等伪影）；教学演示、培训、自学；用自己的 CAD（STEP/STL）体素化后走一遍前向投影—重建全流程。",
    "requirements": "环境需求：无需安装、无需联网、无需 GPU、无需虚拟环境——用 Dragonfly 自带的 numpy/scikit-image/trimesh 在进程内运行。加载 STEP 文件需要 Dragonfly 2027.1 及以上（STL/OBJ/PLY 各版本均可）。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "What it does: a hands-on project that teaches the principles of CT (computed tomography) and of image reconstruction. Three tabs + a Certificate tab: Object/phantom (the attenuation map to 'scan' — create a synthetic phantom, voxelise a STEP/STL mesh, open the 'Create Synthetic CAD Models (STEP)' plugin to design your own, or use a Channel) → Forward projection (set the number of projections / angular range / noise, run the Radon transform to produce projection images and a sinogram, and publish them) → Reconstruction (filtered back-projection FBP with a choice of filter, or iterative SART, compared to the ground-truth object via RMSE). All actions run in-process on numpy/scikit-image, no install/GPU/venv. Few vs many angles and ramp vs none filter vividly demonstrate undersampling streaks and other artifacts.\n\n**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the first tab runs the whole teaching loop unattended - get the object, forward-project it, publish the mid-slice sinogram, reconstruct - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. It does not replace the hand-driven path: every per-step button and every Learn document stays exactly as it is. It is for the second pass, re-running the loop after changing one parameter, and for demonstrating the finished pipeline. Two tick boxes, both OFF by default: 'create a fresh phantom', which does not skip the first step but decides whether it fabricates a new object or scans the one already selected - so a learner who loaded a CAD mesh does not have it replaced - and 'publish the sinogram', which is cheap but adds a teaching Channel the reconstruction does not need. Voxelising a mesh, opening the CAD plugin and saving the certificate PDF all stay out: each needs a file dialog or another plugin's window. Every tab stays open at all times so the parameters can be read and changed BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses at its own button and says which step is missing, instead of the tab being locked shut.",
    "scenarios": "Typical scenarios: understand CT imaging (X-ray attenuation / Beer–Lambert, projections/sinogram, parallel/fan/cone geometry) and reconstruction (why back-projection must be filtered, FBP filter trade-offs, iterative SART, undersampling/limited-angle/beam-hardening artifacts); teaching demos, training, self-study; run the full forward-projection-to-reconstruction pipeline on your own CAD (STEP/STL) voxelised object.",
    "requirements": "Requirements: no install, no internet, no GPU, no venv — runs in-process on Dragonfly's own numpy/scikit-image/trimesh. Loading STEP files needs Dragonfly 2027.1+ (STL/OBJ/PLY work on all versions)."
  }
}

{
  "title_en": "Learning about CT Reconstruction (Hands-on Workshop)",
  "title_cn": "Learning about CT Reconstruction（CT 重建原理 · 上手练习）",
  "menu_en": "Learning about CT Reconstruction...",
  "version": "1.0",
  "date": "2026-07-26",
  "cn": [
    {
      "heading": "简介",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Learning about CT Reconstruction（CT 重建原理）** 是一个用最直观的方式讲清楚 **CT（计算机断层成像）原理**与**图像重建原理**的上手练习项目：为什么绕着物体转一圈、拍很多张 X 光片，就能算出它内部的三维结构。"
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "本功能由第三方（Prototype Labs / Prototype Apps）提供，非 Dragonfly 官方开发或支持，使用风险自负。证书页生成的完成证书亦非官方认证，仅供个人记录。"
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "第一个页签上的**一键执行全部步骤（按当前设置）**会无人值守地跑完整个教学循环：获取被扫描物体 → 正向投影 → 发布中间层正弦图 → 重建，每一步都使用你按下按钮那一刻各自页签上显示的设置。它**不取代**手动操作：每个分步按钮和每篇讲解文档都原样保留。它是为「第二遍」准备的——改一个参数后把整个循环重跑一次——以及用于演示完整流程。两个复选框**默认都关闭**：「生成新的体模」并不是跳过第一步，而是决定这一步究竟是**新造一个物体**还是**扫描当前已选中的物体**，这样学习者自己载入的 CAD 网格就不会被替换掉；「发布正弦图」代价很小，但会多出一个重建并不需要的教学用 Channel。网格体素化、打开 CAD 插件、保存证书 PDF 都不在流程内：它们各自需要文件对话框或另一个插件的窗口。所有页签始终可以打开和修改参数，以便在运行**之前**逐页检查；某一步所需的输入尚不存在时，会在**它自己的按钮**上给出提示，说明缺的是哪一步，而不是把整个页签锁住不让进入。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "三个标签页",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "steps": [
            "**物体 / 体模** —— 我们要“扫描”的对象是一张**衰减分布**。一键生成合成体模（棒/球/环三种多材料体模），或加载并体素化 STEP/STL/OBJ/PLY 网格，或直接调用“创建合成 CAD 模型（STEP）”插件自己设计一个，也可选用已有的 Channel。",
            "**前向投影（采集）** —— 设置 CT 空间参数（投影数 / 角度范围 / 噪声），调用 Radon 变换沿射线积分，生成投影图像（正弦图 sinogram）并发布。这就是真实 CT 采集到的原始数据。",
            "**重建** —— 从投影反算断层：滤波反投影（FBP，可选 ramp / shepp-logan / cosine / hamming / hann / none）或迭代重建（SART），并与体模真值对比 RMSE。"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "会讲清楚的原理",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**成像**：X 射线衰减与 Beer–Lambert 定律，投影记录的是沿射线的线积分。"
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**采集**：正弦图（Radon 变换）、平行/扇/锥束几何、投影数与角度范围的影响。"
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**重建**：反投影为何必须先滤波、FBP 各滤波器的锐利/噪声权衡、迭代 SART 的取舍、以及欠采样条纹/有限角/射束硬化等常见伪影。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "最直观的一课",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "在“前向投影”页把投影数调很小再重建，能看到明显的放射状**欠采样条纹**；调大投影数，条纹消失。把滤波器设为 **none**（纯反投影）会很糊，切回 **ramp** 立刻清晰。这两组对比直观回答了“为什么 CT 要拍很多张、为什么要滤波”。"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "使用步骤",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "steps": [
            "在“物体/体模”页点“生成合成体模”（或体素化一个 CAD 网格 / 选一个 Channel）。",
            "在“前向投影”页设好投影数、角度范围（平行束 180° 即足够），点“生成投影”，发布投影与正弦图。",
            "在“重建”页选方法（FBP / SART）与滤波器，点“重建”，查看重建体与 RMSE。",
            "试试少角度 vs 多角度、ramp vs none，观察画质与 RMSE 的变化。",
            "完成后在“证书”页填写姓名生成完成证书。"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "环境需求",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "无需安装、无需联网、无需 GPU、无需虚拟环境。核心动手环节用 Dragonfly 自带的 numpy/scikit-image/trimesh 在进程内运行（平行束几何，逐层用 Radon 变换）。**加载 STEP 需要 Dragonfly 2027.1 及以上**（STL/OBJ/PLY 各版本均可）。"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "en": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Learning about CT Reconstruction** is a hands-on project that explains, as intuitively as possible, **how CT (computed tomography) works** and **how image reconstruction works**: why rotating around an object and taking many X-ray images lets you compute its internal 3D structure."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "This feature is provided by a third party (Prototype Labs / Apps), not officially developed or supported by Dragonfly. The completion certificate is also unofficial — for personal record only."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the first tab runs the whole teaching loop unattended - get the object, forward-project it, publish the mid-slice sinogram, reconstruct - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. It does not replace the hand-driven path: every per-step button and every Learn document stays exactly as it is. It is for the second pass, re-running the loop after changing one parameter, and for demonstrating the finished pipeline. Two tick boxes, both OFF by default: 'create a fresh phantom', which does not skip the first step but decides whether it fabricates a new object or scans the one already selected - so a learner who loaded a CAD mesh does not have it replaced - and 'publish the sinogram', which is cheap but adds a teaching Channel the reconstruction does not need. Voxelising a mesh, opening the CAD plugin and saving the certificate PDF all stay out: each needs a file dialog or another plugin's window. Every tab stays open at all times so the parameters can be read and changed BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses at its own button and says which step is missing, instead of the tab being locked shut."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The three tabs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "steps": [
            "**Object / phantom** — the thing we 'scan' is an **attenuation map**. Create a synthetic phantom (rods/spheres/rings multi-material phantoms), load and voxelise a STEP/STL/OBJ/PLY mesh, open the 'Create Synthetic CAD Models (STEP)' plugin to design your own, or use an existing Channel.",
            "**Forward projection (acquisition)** — set the CT parameters (number of projections / angular range / noise), run the Radon transform (line integrals along the rays) to produce projection images (a sinogram) and publish them. This is the raw data a real CT records.",
            "**Reconstruction** — recover the cross-sections: filtered back-projection (FBP, filters ramp / shepp-logan / cosine / hamming / hann / none) or iterative SART, compared to the ground-truth object via RMSE."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The principles it teaches",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**Imaging**: X-ray attenuation and the Beer–Lambert law; a projection records a line integral along each ray."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**Acquisition**: the sinogram (Radon transform), parallel/fan/cone geometry, and the effect of the number of angles and the angular range."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**Reconstruction**: why back-projection must be filtered, the sharpness/noise trade-off of the FBP filters, the pros/cons of iterative SART, and common artifacts (undersampling streaks, limited angle, beam hardening)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The most vivid lesson",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "On the 'Forward projection' tab, set a small number of projections and reconstruct — you see clear radial **undersampling streaks**; raise the number and they vanish. Set the filter to **none** (plain back-projection) and it is very blurry; switch back to **ramp** and it is instantly sharp. These two comparisons answer, viscerally, 'why CT takes so many images' and 'why you must filter'."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to use",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "steps": [
            "On 'Object / phantom' click 'Create synthetic phantom' (or voxelise a CAD mesh / pick a Channel).",
            "On 'Forward projection' set the number of projections and range (180° suffices for parallel beam), click 'Generate projections', and publish the projections + sinogram.",
            "On 'Reconstruction' choose the method (FBP / SART) and filter, click 'Reconstruct', and read the volume + RMSE.",
            "Try few vs many angles, and ramp vs none, to watch the quality and RMSE change.",
            "Finish on the 'Certificate' tab by entering your name to generate a completion certificate."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Requirements",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "No install, no internet, no GPU, no venv. The core actions run in-process on Dragonfly's own numpy/scikit-image/trimesh (parallel-beam geometry, slice-by-slice Radon transform). **Loading STEP needs Dragonfly 2027.1+** (STL/OBJ/PLY work on all versions)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Custom Context Menus",
  "cn": {
    "func": "功能：管理你自己添加到 Dragonfly 右键菜单里的菜单项 —— 添加、删除、启用、禁用，都在一个面板里完成，改动不需要重启 Dragonfly（视图画布上立即生效，对象列表上在下次右键时生效），唯一的例外是某一项在插件刚安装后第一次出现。默认内置两项，分别挂在两个不同的右键菜单上。**本插件添加的每一项，在菜单上都会在名称前显示一个标记（默认是圆点 ●），以便与 Dragonfly 原生的菜单项区分开；标记可以在面板「1 菜单项」页的「在 Dragonfly 菜单上标记本插件添加的项」分组里换成 •、▪、@，也可以完全关掉，因此下文引用的一律是不带标记的名称。**第一项「复制当前视图到剪贴板」：在 2D 或 3D 视图画布上右键点击它，就会把**你右键的那个视图**按屏幕分辨率截图并放入系统剪贴板，随后可直接 Ctrl+V 粘贴到报告、邮件、微信或 PPT 里，全程不产生任何临时文件，也不会在 Dragonfly 里生成任何对象。第二项「裁剪并创建子体积」：在**对象列表（数据属性与设置）**中右键“恰好一个”图像、ROI 或 Multi-ROI 时出现，用于快速裁剪 —— 弹出的对话框提供 300×300×300、400×400×400、500×500×500 三个预设尺寸和自定义尺寸，中心默认就是源对象自身的中心（X/Y/Z 偏移均为 0，可按体素为单位修改）。**关键规则：裁剪盒超出源对象的部分，以源对象自身的边界作为新对象的边界，绝不做零填充。**例如源为 1000×1000×450、请求 500×500×500，结果就是 500×500×450；对话框在你点「创建」之前就实时显示最终尺寸、体素总数和预计占用，并明确标出是在哪个轴上被限制了，所以不会有意外。若偏移大到裁剪盒完全落在源对象之外，则直接拒绝并说明该轴的有效偏移范围，而不是悄悄裁出一个 1 体素的对象。裁剪结果作为**新对象**发布，名称形如「<源名称> crop 500x500x450」，源对象保持不变；体素尺寸、坐标轴方向和世界坐标原点全部沿用源对象；4D 数据的所有时间步一起裁剪。面板共三页：「菜单项」增删与启停（并显示每一项的出现条件）；「已发现的菜单」列出当前 Dragonfly 中所有带名字的菜单及其 objectName 与开头几项；「日志」记录每次点击的执行结果。配置保存在 %LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\CustomContextMenus\\items.json，位于插件目录之外，因此重装插件、App Store 更新或换 Dragonfly 版本都不会丢失，并且各版本共用同一份配置。",
    "scenarios": "适用场景：写报告或发消息时需要频繁截取某个视图，不想每次都走「导出视图截图」再选路径、存文件、找文件；给同事或客户快速发一张当前视图；从一套大数据里快速切出一个固定尺寸的立方体子体积做试算、做深度学习训练块、或截取感兴趣区域再做分析，而不必打开完整的裁剪工具逐个填索引；把将来陆续需要的自定义右键功能集中在一处管理，而不是每加一个功能就多装一个插件。",
    "requirements": "环境需求：无需安装、联网、GPU 或虚拟环境，纯进程内 PyQt6。截图走 Dragonfly 自己的视图导出接口（与「导出视图截图」同一条路径），因此 OpenGL 渲染的三维内容也能正常截取。裁剪走 Dragonfly 自己的 StructuredGrid.getSubset —— 与官方「Crop…」相同的原生调用，因此图像、ROI 和 Multi-ROI 都只用一次调用完成，几何信息自动继承，不需要也不会重采样。注意本插件的两项使用**两套不同机制**：画布右键菜单由 Dragonfly 原生代码构建，只能向活动的 QMenu 注入菜单项；对象列表右键菜单才是 Dragonfly 官方的 Python 上下文菜单通道，所以「裁剪并创建子体积」用的是插件自带的 @menuItem() 注册方式，能精确限定“恰好一个图像/ROI/Multi-ROI”。因此该项在**首次安装后需要重启一次 Dragonfly**（Dragonfly 只在启动时读取插件菜单），之后再勾选或取消勾选立即生效。选中多个对象时该项不显示，原因会写进 Python 日志：对话框只能预览一个结果，而多个对象的尺寸通常不同，同一个裁剪盒对每个对象的含义并不相同。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "What it does: manages the entries you add to Dragonfly's own context menus — add, remove, enable, disable, all from one panel, and no change needs a Dragonfly restart — immediately on the view canvas, at your next right-click on the object list — the one exception being an entry appearing for the very first time after installation. Two entries ship enabled, on two DIFFERENT context menus. EVERY entry this plugin adds is displayed with a marker in front of its name on the menu — a dot (●) by default — so it is distinguishable from Dragonfly's own entries, which never carry one; the marker can be changed (•, ▪, @) or switched off completely in the 'Mark our entries on Dragonfly's menus' group of the panel's '1 Entries' tab, so the names quoted below are the plain names, without it. The first, 'Copy current view to clipboard': right-click a 2D or 3D view canvas and click it, and a screenshot of THE VIEW YOU RIGHT-CLICKED, at its own screen resolution, goes straight to the system clipboard — ready to paste into a report, an email, a chat or a slide. No temporary file is written and no Dragonfly object is created. The second, 'Crop and create a sub-volume': it appears when you right-click exactly ONE image, ROI or Multi-ROI in the OBJECT LIST (Data Properties and Settings), and it exists for fast cropping. The dialog offers three preset sizes — 300x300x300, 400x400x400, 500x500x500 — plus a custom size, and a centre that defaults to the source object's own centre (X/Y/Z offsets of 0, editable in voxels). THE KEY RULE: where the requested box falls outside the source, the source's own boundary becomes the boundary of the new object, and nothing is zero-padded. A 1000x1000x450 source with a 500x500x500 request gives 500x500x450 — and the dialog shows that final extent, the total voxel count and the estimated size BEFORE you commit, naming the axis that was clamped, so it is never a surprise. An offset large enough to push the box completely outside the source is REFUSED with the valid offset range for that axis, rather than quietly producing a 1-voxel object. The result is published as a NEW object named '<source> crop 500x500x450'; the source is left untouched, voxel size, axis directions and world origin are inherited, and every time step of a 4-D dataset is cropped. The panel has three tabs: 'Entries' to add/remove and enable/disable, with a column stating when each entry appears; 'Discovered menus', which lists every named menu alive in the running Dragonfly with its objectName and first few items; and 'Log', which records the result of every click. The configuration lives in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\CustomContextMenus\\items.json — outside the plugin folder, so it survives a plugin reinstall, an App Store update and a new Dragonfly version, and is shared by every installed version.",
    "scenarios": "Typical scenarios: you grab the same view repeatedly while writing a report, and do not want to go through 'Export Screenshot of View', pick a folder, save a file and then find it again; you want to send a colleague or a customer the view you are looking at, right now; you want to cut a fixed-size cube out of a large dataset for a trial run, a deep-learning training block, or a region of interest to analyse, without opening a full cropping tool and typing index ranges; you want the custom right-click features you will keep adding managed in one place instead of installing another plugin for each one.",
    "requirements": "Requirements: no install, no internet, no GPU, no venv — pure in-process PyQt6. The capture uses Dragonfly's own view-export interface (the same route as 'Export Screenshot of View'), so OpenGL-rendered 3-D content is captured correctly. The crop uses Dragonfly's own StructuredGrid.getSubset — the same native call as the built-in 'Crop...' — so an image, a ROI and a Multi-ROI are each handled by one call, geometry is inherited, and nothing is resampled. Note that the two entries use TWO DIFFERENT mechanisms: the view-canvas menu is built by Dragonfly's native code, so an entry there has to be injected into the live QMenu, whereas the object-list menu IS Dragonfly's supported Python contextual-menu channel — so 'Crop and create a sub-volume' is registered as the plugin's own @menuItem(), which is what lets it appear for exactly one image, ROI or Multi-ROI and for nothing else. Because of that, this entry needs ONE Dragonfly restart after first installation (Dragonfly reads a plugin's menus only at startup); enabling and disabling it afterwards is immediate. With several objects selected the entry is not shown, and the reason is written to the Python log: the dialog previews one result, and objects rarely share an extent, so the same box would mean a different crop for each of them."
  }
}

{
  "title_en": "Custom Context Menus",
  "title_cn": "Custom Context Menus（自定义右键菜单）",
  "menu_en": "Custom Context Menus...",
  "version": "1.1",
  "date": "2026-08-10",
  "en": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Adds your own entries to **Dragonfly's own context menus** and manages them from one place: add, remove, enable, disable. Changes need no Dragonfly restart: on the view canvas they take effect immediately, and on the object list at your next right-click. The one thing that does need a restart is an entry appearing for the **very first time**, right after the plugin is installed or updated — see *Installation & enabling*."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Two entries ship enabled, on two different menus.** *Copy current view to clipboard* is on the 2D/3D **view canvas**: right-click a canvas, click it, and a screenshot of the view you right-clicked - at its own size on screen - is on the system clipboard, ready to paste into a report, an email, a chat or a slide. *Crop and create a sub-volume* is on the **object list** (Data Properties and Settings): right-click one image, ROI or Multi-ROI and cut a preset-sized block out of it in two clicks."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**A marker shows which entries are ours - and this manual quotes names without it.** Every entry this plugin adds is displayed with one glyph in front of its name, `●` by default, so you can see at a glance that Prototype Apps added it and Dragonfly did not; Dragonfly's own entries never carry one. So the first entry above is named *Copy current view to clipboard*, and with the default marker it reads `● Copy current view to clipboard` on the menu. **Every label quoted in this manual is the name, without the marker.** That is deliberate: the marker is a display prefix only, and you can change it (`•`, `▪`, `@`) or switch it off completely in the **Mark our entries on Dragonfly's menus** group of the *1 Entries* tab, so the plain name is the one form that is correct whichever setting you chose - and it is also what the panel's own list of entries shows. Changing it needs no restart, and the two routes differ by one right-click: the view-canvas entries are re-labelled immediately, and the object-list entry picks the new marker up the next time you right-click."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**The source is never modified.** Cropping publishes a NEW object and leaves the original exactly as it was."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**No file, no object.** Nothing is written to disk and nothing appears in Dragonfly's object tree. The temporary image the capture needs is deleted immediately."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Use cases",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Grabbing the same view over and over while writing a report, without going through *Export Screenshot of View*, choosing a folder, saving a file and then finding it again."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Sending a colleague or a customer the view you are looking at, right now, in a chat window."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Cutting a fixed-size cube out of a large dataset - a trial run before committing to a long computation, a training block for deep learning, or a region of interest to analyse on its own - without opening a full cropping tool and typing index ranges."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Keeping the custom right-click features you will keep adding managed in one place, instead of installing a separate plugin for each one."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Installation & enabling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "items": [
            "Open **Prototype Apps ▸ App Store**, find *Custom Context Menus* in the **Menu Manager** group, and enable it.",
            "Restart Dragonfly. Plugins are discovered at startup, so a restart is required.",
            "Nothing else is needed. The engine starts with Dragonfly and adds the enabled entries by itself; the panel does not have to be opened first.",
            "The panel is at **Prototype Apps ▸ Custom Context Menus...** if you want to add, remove or turn entries off.",
            "**Crop and create a sub-volume** appears in the object list's right-click menu after that restart. Dragonfly reads a plugin's menus only when it starts, so the very first appearance needs the restart; ticking and unticking the entry afterwards is immediate, like every other entry."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "No install, no internet, no GPU, no virtual environment — pure in-process PyQt6."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Using the clipboard entry",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "items": [
            "Right-click anywhere on a 2D or 3D view canvas.",
            "Click **Copy current view to clipboard** - shown with the marker in front of it, unless you switched the marker off - at the bottom of the menu below a separator.",
            "Paste with Ctrl+V into whatever you are writing."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The captured image is the view's actual size on screen, in device pixels. Right-clicking a view canvas makes that view the current view, which is how the entry knows which of several views you meant."
        },
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "If the captured image looks blank the Log tab says so instead of leaving you to discover it after pasting. A view that has just been created or is mid-render can capture flat; right-click and copy again."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Cropping a sub-volume",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Select **one** image, ROI or Multi-ROI in the object list (Data Properties and Settings), right-click it, and choose **Crop and create a sub-volume** - again shown with the marker in front of it, unless you switched the marker off. It sits with Dragonfly's own crop entries under *Modify and Transform*."
        },
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "items": [
            "Pick a size: **300 x 300 x 300**, **400 x 400 x 400**, **500 x 500 x 500**, or **Custom** for any size per axis.",
            "Leave the centre alone to crop around the source's own centre, or set the X / Y / Z offsets. The offsets are in **voxels**: +1 on X moves the block one voxel towards higher X.",
            "Read the **What will be created** line. It updates as you type and gives the exact extent, the voxel count, the approximate size, and whether the source boundary decided any axis.",
            "Press **Create the sub-volume**. The new object appears in the object list, named *<source> crop 500x500x450*."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "h3",
          "text": "What happens when the box does not fit"
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**The source's own boundary becomes the boundary of the new object, and nothing is padded.** The result is the overlap between what you asked for and what exists, so it contains only real data."
        },
        {
          "kind": "table",
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Source",
              "Requested",
              "Offset",
              "Result"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "1000 x 1000 x 450",
                "500 x 500 x 500",
                "0, 0, 0",
                "**500 x 500 x 450** (clamped on Z)"
              ],
              [
                "300 x 200 x 100",
                "500 x 500 x 500",
                "0, 0, 0",
                "300 x 200 x 100 (the whole source)"
              ],
              [
                "1000 x 1000 x 450",
                "300 x 300 x 300",
                "400, 0, 0",
                "250 x 300 x 300 (clamped on X)"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The dialog states this before you commit, naming the axis, so a smaller result is something you read rather than discover afterwards."
        },
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "If an offset is large enough to push the box **completely outside** the source, the crop is **refused** and the dialog says which axis and which offsets would work. It is not quietly clamped down to a sliver - a box at 2250..2749 in a 1000-voxel axis would otherwise produce a 1-voxel object that looks like a successful crop."
        },
        {
          "kind": "h3",
          "text": "What is preserved"
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Voxel size, axis directions and the world origin are inherited from the source, so the sub-volume sits exactly where that part of the source sat and measurements stay comparable. Nothing is resampled or interpolated: the crop is Dragonfly's own native subset operation, the same one the built-in *Crop...* uses."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "A Multi-ROI keeps its label names, colours and scalar values. A 4-D dataset is cropped on **every** time step, and the dialog says how many there are."
        },
        {
          "kind": "h3",
          "text": "Why only one object at a time"
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The entry appears for exactly one image, ROI or Multi-ROI, and for nothing else - not for a mesh, not for a graph, not for an annotation, and not for a multi-selection. The dialog previews one result, and two objects rarely have the same extent, so the same box would mean a different crop for each of them and the preview would be right for at most one. Dragonfly's own *Crop...* works the same way. When several objects that could be cropped are selected, the reason is written to Dragonfly's Python log."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Interface",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
