  "en": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**ImageJ / Fiji Bridge** connects Dragonfly to the **Fiji/ImageJ** you already have installed: pick **published** Dragonfly objects in the panel, choose a built-in command or write a macro, and the plugin exports those objects as ImageJ TIFFs, drives your local Fiji **headlessly** to process them, and brings the results back into Dragonfly."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "Since version 2.0 a single run can take **several inputs**, a chosen **range of time points**, and **MultiROI/ROI labels**, and can bring back three things at once: a new **Channel**, a new **MultiROI**, and ImageJ's **measurements table**."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "The mechanism is a **job folder**: the plugin creates one folder per run, writes the inputs there under fixed names, and the macro only needs to know that folder."},
        {"kind": "table", "table": {"headers": ["File", "Direction", "Meaning"], "rows": [["`in_1.tif` … `in_N.tif`", "→ ImageJ", "image inputs, in the order you arranged them"], ["`labels_1.tif` …", "→ ImageJ", "a MultiROI (label ids kept) or an ROI (0/255 mask)"], ["`job.json`", "→ ImageJ", "titles, voxel spacing, time-point count, data type"], ["`out.tif`", "← ImageJ", "result image (may be a 4-D hyperstack) → a Channel"], ["`out_labels.tif`", "← ImageJ", "result label image → a **MultiROI**"], ["`results.csv`", "← ImageJ", "the ImageJ Results table → the panel's Results tab"], ["`runner_log.txt`", "← ImageJ", "everything Fiji printed; the first place to look"]]}},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "The older `<in>|<out>` contract still works exactly as before, so macros you wrote and saved in \"My Macros\" keep running. Which contract a macro wants is **declared by the macro** (`// @protocol job` or `// @protocol legacy`) rather than guessed."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Use cases",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Reusing ImageJ/Fiji's huge ecosystem (plugins, macros, algorithms) directly: life-science microscopy, materials image processing."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Two-input arithmetic**: subtract a background acquisition, divide by a flat field, difference two time points - with Image Calculator."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Numbers, not pixels**: Analyze Particles per object, or per-slice intensity statistics, shown as a table you can save as CSV."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Labelled objects**: turn a threshold result into one object per particle and bring it back as a MultiROI for Dragonfly's own measurement and display tools."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Time series**: process only the time points you care about in a 4-D image, saving both memory and time."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**A MultiROI as a mask**: keep only what the labels cover and zero the rest."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Installation & enabling",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "p", "text": "The plugin ships with Prototype Apps. Enable it with the installer (`install_prototype_apps.exe`) or from the **App Store** inside Dragonfly; the menu entry is **Prototype Apps ▸ Detection & Bridges ▸ ImageJ / Fiji Bridge...**."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "Menus are built when Dragonfly **starts**, so **restart Dragonfly once** after installing or enabling it."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Runtime environment & first-run setup",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "p", "text": "There is **no** Python virtual environment to build and nothing to download: TIFF I/O uses Dragonfly's bundled `tifffile` (falling back to Pillow). The only external dependency is a **locally installed Fiji/ImageJ**, free from https://fiji.sc."},
        {"kind": "steps", "steps": ["Open the panel and look at the **Fiji / ImageJ** group. The plugin searches the usual locations, so it is often already filled in.", "If not, click **Browse...** and pick the launcher inside your Fiji installation. **Prefer `ImageJ-win64.exe`.**", "Raise **Max heap** (default 4096 MB) for large volumes.", "The path and heap size are remembered in `imagej_config.json` next to the plugin code."]},
        {"kind": "note", "text": "Why `ImageJ-win64.exe` matters: the newer Jaunch launcher (`fiji-windows-x64.exe` / `fiji.exe`) does **not** run a headless macro - it reports success having done nothing. When the plugin sees a Jaunch launcher it switches to the `ImageJ-win64.exe` sibling in the same Fiji.app and says so in the log."},
        {"kind": "note", "text": "The environment variable `DF_FIJI_PATH` can also point at the launcher."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Interface",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "h3", "text": "Inputs"},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "The list at the top is this run's inputs. Each row shows which job file it becomes (`in_1.tif` / `labels_1.tif`), its type, name, shape, time-point count and label count. The combo below lists every **published** Channel, MultiROI and ROI in the session; **Add** puts one in the list and **Move up/Move down** reorder it - the order decides which object is `in_1`, which is the contract with the macro. Images and MultiROIs/ROIs are numbered in separate sequences."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "Selecting a 4-D (time series) row enables the **time points** row: **All**, or an explicit from/to range. The range belongs to that input, so selecting another row and coming back keeps what you set."},
        {"kind": "h3", "text": "Operation"},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "Three ways: a **built-in command** (a common ImageJ operation with parameters), a **custom macro** (load a preset from the library and edit it), or a **macro file** (.ijm). A built-in command states how many inputs it needs, whether it needs a MultiROI, and whether it changes the data type."},
        {"kind": "h3", "text": "Bring back"},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "Three tick boxes: image → new Channel, labels → new MultiROI, measurements table. Choosing a built-in command **ticks exactly what that command can produce and disables the rest** - a measurement-only command will not pretend it can hand you an image."},
        {"kind": "h3", "text": "Running and diagnosing"},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**Run in Fiji + Import** starts the run, **Cancel** stops it, and **Open job folder** opens this run's folder (the exported inputs, the macro that actually ran, and `runner_log.txt`). The two tabs below hold the log and the Results table; the table can be saved as CSV."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to use",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "steps", "steps": ["Prepare your data in Dragonfly and make sure the objects are **published** (unpublished objects are deliberately not offered).", "Open **Prototype Apps ▸ Detection & Bridges ▸ ImageJ / Fiji Bridge...**.", "Check the Fiji launcher path.", "Pick an object in the combo and click **Add**; repeat for a second image or a label input, and reorder if needed.", "For a 4-D input, select its row and set the time-point range if you do not want all of them.", "Choose a built-in command and its parameters, or switch to a custom macro and load a preset to edit.", "Check the **Bring back** ticks and the hint under the input list - it tells you what is still missing.", "Click **Run in Fiji + Import**. Output streams into the Log tab while Fiji works.", "When it finishes, the new Channel / MultiROI appear in Dragonfly's object list and the table appears in the Results tab."]}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Parameters",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "table", "table": {"headers": ["Parameter", "Meaning"], "rows": [["Launcher", "Path to the Fiji/ImageJ launcher. Prefer `ImageJ-win64.exe`."], ["Max heap", "Maximum JVM heap for Fiji in MB; 0 uses the launcher default. Raise it for big volumes."], ["Output suffix", "Suffix of the result name, which becomes `<input> - <suffix>`."], ["Time points All / from / to", "Which time points of that input take part (1-based, inclusive)."], ["Threshold method", "Auto-threshold method (Otsu, Huang, Li, MaxEntropy, Mean, Triangle, Yen, Default), named as in ImageJ."], ["Min size (px)", "Smallest object Analyze Particles keeps, in pixels."], ["Operation", "The Image Calculator operation: Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide, AND, OR, XOR, Min, Max, Average, Difference."], ["Sigma / Radius / Iterations", "Each filter's own parameters, with the same meaning as in the identically named ImageJ command."]]}},
        {"kind": "h3", "text": "Variables a macro can use"},
        {"kind": "table", "table": {"headers": ["Variable", "Meaning"], "rows": [["`dir`", "the job folder, with a trailing slash"], ["`nIn`", "how many `in_*.tif` image inputs exist"], ["`nLab`", "how many `labels_*.tif` label inputs exist"], ["`saveOut`", "1 by default: save the active image as `out.tif` at the end. Set it to 0 in a measurement-only macro, or when the real result is `out_labels.tif`"]]}},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "If the macro measured anything (`nResults > 0`) the Results table is saved to `results.csv` **automatically** - no `saveAs` line needed."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Output",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**A new Channel** (from `out.tif`), named `<input> - <suffix>`. **Integer results stay integral** (8/16-bit is not forced to float), voxel spacing and origin are carried over where possible, and a 4-D result keeps its time axis."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**A new MultiROI** (from `out_labels.tif`): labels are renumbered to a consecutive 1..N and given default colours."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**A measurements table** (from `results.csv`): shown in the Results tab with numeric columns right-aligned, and saveable as CSV."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**The job folder**: the exported inputs, `job.json`, the `macro.ijm` that actually ran and `runner_log.txt` all stay on disk whether the run succeeded or failed - that is what you diagnose from."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "FAQ & troubleshooting",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**Fiji is not found.** Browse to `ImageJ-win64.exe` yourself, or set `DF_FIJI_PATH`. If you have no Fiji yet, download it from https://fiji.sc."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**Fiji \"succeeds\" but produces nothing.** Almost always the Jaunch launcher (`fiji-windows-x64.exe`). Use `ImageJ-win64.exe` from the same folder; the log and the error message say so too."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**It says none of out.tif / out_labels.tif / results.csv was produced.** Open the job folder and read `runner_log.txt`: usually a mistyped command or option, a command that needs a display (unavailable headless), or a missing Fiji plugin."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**A MorphoLibJ preset reports an unknown command.** Enable the **IJPB-plugins** update site in Fiji (Help ▸ Update...), restart Fiji, and try again."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**The imported image looks like one colour only.** A Dragonfly Channel holds one value per voxel, so when ImageJ returns a colour image (e.g. the tagged skeleton of Analyze Skeleton) only channel 1 can be kept, and the log says so. Split the channels in your macro and output them separately if you need all three."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**The label import fails saying there are no labels.** Every voxel in `out_labels.tif` is 0, i.e. the segmentation found nothing - adjust the threshold method or the minimum size."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**A long time series runs out of memory.** Use the time-point range and process it in batches: export only the frames you need."},
        {"kind": "p", "text": "**The run is refused because the TIFF library failed its self-test.** Before every run the plugin round-trips known arrays through TIFF; if that fails it stops rather than writing data it cannot read back. Please send us that log section."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Notes & known limitations",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Orientation matrices are not carried**: this version transfers voxel spacing and origin only. If your data has a rotated or sheared orientation, the result's orientation is not restored."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "One run brings back at most **one** MultiROI (`out_labels.tif`) and **one** Channel (`out.tif`)."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**A MultiROI has no time axis**: if the label result is 4-D, only its first time point is used, and the log says so."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "A macro declaring the `legacy` contract only receives the **first** Channel; rewrite it against the job contract (`dir` + `in_1.tif`) to use several inputs."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "An ROI is exported as a **0/255 mask** and a MultiROI as **label ids** - which matches what ImageJ's binary commands expect, but is a difference to keep in mind when writing macros."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "ImageJ commands that cannot run headless (needing a window or interaction) cannot run here either."},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "The plugin never installs or updates Fiji, and never enables an update site for you."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "References",
      "blocks": [
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Fiji / ImageJ: https://fiji.sc ; the ImageJ macro language: https://imagej.net/ij/developer/macro/functions.html"},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Particle analysis: https://imagej.net/imaging/particle-analysis"},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Image Calculator: https://imagej.net/ij/docs/menus/process.html#calculator"},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "MorphoLibJ: https://imagej.net/plugins/morpholibj ; Legland et al. 2016, doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btw413"},
        {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Running ImageJ headless: https://imagej.net/learn/headless"}
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "From Image to STL: The Basics (Hands-on Workshop)",
  "cn": {
    "func": "功能：面向最初级用户的上手练习项目，讲清楚并动手跑通最基础的完整流程——从三维图像到 STL 模型。五个标签页 + 证书页：概念（完整流程、像素/体素、灰度、分辨率/单位）→ 加载图像（一键生成球/圆柱/带孔立方体/哑铃等简单示例图像，或选用一个已加载的 Channel）→ 阈值分割生成 ROI（手动设范围，或 Otsu 自动阈值，创建并显示真实的 Dragonfly ROI）→ ROI 转表面网格（marching cubes，发布为 Dragonfly 网格）→ 导出 STL（trimesh 写出 .stl，可选二进制/ASCII）。每一步都既有“一键完成”的操作按钮，也有“在 Dragonfly 界面里怎么做”的手把手弹窗，并尽可能多地用弹窗解释基础概念（分割、阈值、Otsu、直方图、ROI、网格、marching cubes、STL、单位等）。全部动手环节在进程内用 numpy/scikit-image/trimesh 运行，无需安装/GPU/联网。\n\n「1 · 概念」页上的 Execute All Steps with Current Settings（按当前设置执行全部步骤） 会无人值守地把整个流程跑一遍：加载图像 → 阈值分割为 ROI → 生成表面网格，每一步都使用你按下按钮那一刻它自己那一页显示的设置。它会向会话写入对象：阈值分割发布一个 ROI，生成网格发布一个 Mesh；勾选该分组里的选项还会额外发布一个示例 Channel（默认不勾选，因为它会替换后续每一步所用的图像）。写出 STL 文件不在本次运行之内：它会弹出「保存」对话框，而无人值守的运行无法回答对话框，请在运行结束后到「5 · 导出 STL」页自己点一下。它不取代逐页动手操作——每一个步骤按钮、每一份讲解文档都原样保留，它适合「第二遍」（改一个参数重跑全程）和讲师一键演示。所有标签页始终可用；某一步所需的输入尚不存在时，会在它自己的按钮上明确拒绝，并告诉你要先去哪一页。",
    "scenarios": "适用场景：刚接触三维图像处理的初学者，快速建立“图像→分割→网格→STL”的整体认知；教学演示、培训、自学；把自己的图像（Channel）走一遍最基础的逆向/3D 打印前处理流程；理解手动阈值与 Otsu 的差别、体素级与亚体素网格的差别、STL 的 ASCII/二进制与单位陷阱。",
    "requirements": "环境需求：无需安装、无需联网、无需 GPU、无需虚拟环境——用 Dragonfly 自带的 numpy/scikit-image/trimesh 在进程内运行。加载 STEP 不在本课范围内；导出为 STL/OBJ/PLY 用 trimesh，各版本均可。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "What it does: a beginner-focused hands-on project that teaches and runs the most basic complete workflow — from a 3D image to an STL model. Five tabs + a Certificate tab: Concepts (the pipeline, pixels/voxels, greyscale, resolution/units) → Load image (one-click sample image: ball/cylinder/cube-with-hole/dumbbell, or use a loaded Channel) → Threshold to a ROI (manual range or Otsu auto-threshold, creating and showing a real Dragonfly ROI) → ROI to surface mesh (marching cubes, published as a Dragonfly mesh) → Export STL (trimesh writes the .stl, binary or ASCII). Every step pairs a one-click action button with a step-by-step 'how to do this in the Dragonfly GUI' pop-up, and uses as many pop-ups as possible to explain the basic concepts (segmentation, threshold, Otsu, histogram, ROI, mesh, marching cubes, STL, units, …). All actions run in-process on numpy/scikit-image/trimesh — no install/GPU/internet.\n\nExecute All Steps with Current Settings on the Concepts tab runs the whole pipeline unattended - load the image, threshold it to a ROI, build the surface mesh - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. It DOES write to your session: the threshold publishes a ROI and the meshing publishes a Mesh, and ticking the box in that group publishes a sample Channel as well (OFF by default, because it replaces the image every later step works on). Writing the STL file is NOT part of the run - it opens a Save dialog that an unattended run cannot answer - so press Export STL on Step 5 when the run finishes. It does not replace working through the tabs: every step button and every Learn document stays exactly as it is, and it is meant for the second pass (re-running everything after changing one parameter) and for an instructor demonstrating the finished pipeline. Every tab stays open, and a step whose input does not exist yet refuses AT ITS OWN BUTTON, naming the step you have to run first.",
    "scenarios": "Typical scenarios: absolute beginners building an overall mental model of 'image → segmentation → mesh → STL'; teaching demos, training, self-study; running the most basic reverse-engineering / 3D-print pre-processing on your own image (Channel); understanding manual vs Otsu thresholding, voxel-level vs sub-voxel meshing, and STL's ASCII/binary and units pitfalls.",
    "requirements": "Requirements: no install, no internet, no GPU, no venv — runs in-process on Dragonfly's own numpy/scikit-image/trimesh. STEP loading is out of scope here; STL/OBJ/PLY export uses trimesh and works on all versions."
  }
}

{
  "title_en": "From Image to STL: The Basics (Hands-on Workshop)",
