  "en": [
    {"heading": "Introduction", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "**From Image to STL: The Basics** is a hands-on project for **absolute beginners** that teaches and runs the most basic, most common complete workflow end to end: **from a single 3D image, step by step, to a 3D-printable STL model**."},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the Concepts tab runs the whole pipeline unattended - load the image, threshold it to a ROI, build the surface mesh - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. It DOES write to your session: the threshold publishes a ROI and the meshing publishes a Mesh, and ticking the box in that group publishes a sample Channel as well (OFF by default, because it replaces the image every later step works on). Writing the STL file is NOT part of the run - it opens a Save dialog that an unattended run cannot answer - so press Export STL on Step 5 when the run finishes. It does not replace working through the tabs: every step button and every Learn document stays exactly as it is, and it is meant for the second pass (re-running everything after changing one parameter) and for an instructor demonstrating the finished pipeline. Every tab stays open, and a step whose input does not exist yet refuses AT ITS OWN BUTTON, naming the step you have to run first."},
      {"kind": "note", "text": "This feature is provided by a third party (Prototype Labs / Apps), not officially developed or supported by Dragonfly. The completion certificate is also unofficial — for personal record only."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "The whole pipeline (4 steps)", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "table", "table": {"headers": ["Step", "Input → Output", "In a nutshell"], "rows": [
        ["1. Load image", "file → greyscale volume", "read the 3D image in"],
        ["2. Threshold", "greyscale volume → ROI (binary)", "pick the object out by brightness"],
        ["3. To a mesh", "ROI → triangle mesh", "wrap a surface around the picked region"],
        ["4. Export STL", "mesh → .stl file", "save as a universal 3D format"]
      ]}}
    ]},
    {"heading": "The five tabs", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "steps", "steps": [
        "**Concepts** — the pipeline, pixels/voxels, grey values, resolution & units.",
        "**Load image** — one-click sample image (ball/cylinder/cube-with-hole/dumbbell), or use a loaded Channel.",
        "**Threshold → ROI** — manual range or Otsu auto-threshold; creates and shows a real Dragonfly ROI.",
        "**ROI → Mesh** — marching cubes builds a triangular surface mesh, published as a Dragonfly mesh.",
        "**Export STL** — trimesh writes the .stl file (binary or ASCII)."
      ]}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Two design features", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**One-click AND learn-by-hand**: every step has a '▶ action button' that actually does it, plus a '🖱 How to do this in the Dragonfly GUI' pop-up that walks you through doing the same thing by hand in Dragonfly (menu paths, panels, right-click items)."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**As many concept pop-ups as possible**: segmentation, threshold, Otsu, histogram, ROI, surface mesh, marching cubes, voxel-level vs sub-voxel, STL, ASCII vs binary, units pitfalls — each has a short, beginner-friendly '📖 Learn' pop-up."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "How to use", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "steps", "steps": [
        "On 'Load image' click 'Create a sample image' (or pick a Channel).",
        "On 'Threshold' click 'Otsu auto-threshold' (or set low/high by hand) to make a ROI.",
        "On 'ROI → Mesh' click 'Convert ROI to a surface mesh'.",
        "On 'Export STL' click 'Export STL…' and choose where to save.",
        "Finish on the 'Certificate' tab by entering your name to generate a completion certificate."
      ]}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Requirements", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "No install, no internet, no GPU, no venv. The core actions run in-process on Dragonfly's own numpy/scikit-image/trimesh (thresholding makes a real ROI via getAsROIWithinRange, marching cubes builds the mesh, trimesh writes the STL). STL/OBJ/PLY export works on all versions."}
    ]}
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Load Images (ITK/SimpleITK)",
  "cn": {
