  "en": [
    {"heading": "Introduction", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "**Load Sanying CT Image Files** imports **Sanying (三英) micro-CT reconstructed RAW volumes** into Dragonfly. After reconstruction the `Recon` folder typically holds `FdkRecon-<type>-<X>x<Y>x<Z>.raw` (default `ushort` = 16-bit unsigned) and a `Reconstruct.txt`. The plugin auto-parses these, previews a mid-slice, and loads the volume as an image Channel."},
      {"kind": "note", "text": "This feature is provided by a third party (Prototype Labs / Apps), not officially developed or supported by Dragonfly. Use at your own risk."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "How dimensions & parameters are determined", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "Parameters come from the `[ReconstructItem]` section of `Reconstruct.txt` plus the raw filename:"},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**X = VoxelX, Y = VoxelY** (always from the txt)."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Z**: for a circle-trajectory scan (`CircleNum = 1`) use `VoxelZ`; for a **helical** scan (`CircleNum ≠ 1`, e.g. 4) `VoxelZ` is wrong, so Z is the **3rd integer in the raw filename**."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Voxel size = VoxelDis** (millimetres, isotropic)."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "**Data type** defaults to 16-bit unsigned (the `ushort` in the filename)."},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "The plugin also cross-checks against the **file size** and auto-corrects Z if needed. Every parameter is editable in the panel."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "How to use", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "steps", "steps": [
        "Click 'Browse File…' to pick `FdkRecon-*.raw`, or 'Browse Folder…' to pick the `Recon` folder (the raw is found automatically).",
        "Check the auto-detected dimensions / data type / byte order / voxel size / scan mode (edit if needed).",
        "For very large volumes, pick a **Downsample** factor or set **Z from / Z to** to load a sub-range; the estimated loaded size is shown.",
        "Use 'Preview mid-slice' to confirm the read is correct.",
        "Click '**Load into Dragonfly**' — the volume appears as a new image Channel in the object list."
      ]}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Requirements", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "No install, no internet, no GPU, no venv. Reads with Dragonfly's own numpy via **memory-mapping**, slice-by-slice for big volumes; downsampling is stride subsampling, so peak memory ≈ one slice + the output volume."}
    ]}
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Load TXM Files",
  "cn": {
    "func": "在 Dragonfly 内打开蔡司（ZEISS）Xradia 的 .txm 重建体数据文件，并发布为 Dragonfly image Channel。弹出窗口中：用「Add Files...」选择一个或多个 .txm 文件，或用「Add Folder...」递归扫描某个文件夹下的所有 .txm；点选列表中的某个文件即可看到它的尺寸（X×Y×Z）、数据类型、体素尺寸（由 TXM 的 PixelSize 读出，单位微米）以及中间切片的预览缩略图；点击「Load into Dragonfly」把所选文件全部读入并发布为 Channel（体素 spacing 按物理尺寸写入，单位米）。解码逻辑直接沿用 UnitedVision 项目中已验证可用的 TXM 读取代码——.txm 本质是 OLE2 复合文档，逐个 ImageDataN/ImageM 流读取切片——而不使用任何 Dragonfly 内置加载器。",
    "scenarios": "适用于把蔡司 Xradia / Versa / Context 等显微 CT 设备输出的 .txm 重建体数据导入 Dragonfly，尤其是当希望用与 UnitedVision 完全一致的读取路径（相同的体素尺寸与切片顺序）来保证结果可复现时。也可用于批量导入一个文件夹下的多个 .txm。",
    "requirements": "纯 Python 进程内运行，无需建立 venv、无需联网、无需 GPU：仅依赖 Dragonfly 自带的 numpy 和随插件打包的纯 Python olefile（OLE2 读取器）。一个约 990 张切片的 uint16 体数据（约 2 GB）解码约需数十秒。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Opens ZEISS Xradia .txm reconstructed volume files inside Dragonfly and publishes them as Dragonfly image Channels. In the popup: use 'Add Files...' to pick one or more .txm files, or 'Add Folder...' to recursively scan a folder for every .txm; select a file in the list to see its dimensions (X x Y x Z), data type, voxel size (read from the TXM PixelSize, in micrometres) and a mid-slice preview thumbnail; click 'Load into Dragonfly' to read all listed files and publish them as Channels (voxel spacing written from the physical size, in metres). The decode logic is carried over verbatim from the UnitedVision project's working TXM reader - a .txm is an OLE2 compound file, read slice-by-slice from its ImageDataN/ImageM streams - and does NOT use any Dragonfly built-in loader.",
    "scenarios": "For importing .txm reconstructed volumes from ZEISS Xradia / Versa / Context micro-CT instruments into Dragonfly, especially when you want the exact same read path as UnitedVision (identical voxel spacing and slice order) for reproducibility. Also handy for batch-importing many .txm files from a folder.",
    "requirements": "Runs purely in-process with no venv, no internet and no GPU: it needs only Dragonfly's bundled numpy plus a pure-Python olefile (OLE2 reader) packaged with the plugin. Decoding a ~990-slice uint16 volume (~2 GB) takes on the order of tens of seconds."
