       [
        "Typical examples",
        "local thickness, mean curvature, distance to something",
        "volume, surface area or equivalent diameter per particle"
       ],
       [
        "What the statistics mean",
        "the distribution of values over the surface",
        "the distribution across that population of objects"
       ],
       [
        "Where it lands in the report",
        "sheets/slides prefixed `Vertex-`",
        "sheets/slides prefixed `Label-`, plus a per-label value listing"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "For example: a **vertex-based** mean thickness of 12 µm says \"averaged over the surface, the material is 12 µm thick\"; a **label-based** mean volume of 6 mm³ says \"these particles average 6 mm³ each\". The two cannot be compared or combined, so the overview places them in **two separate blocks (xlsx) / on two separate slides (pptx)** and every N is labelled as either a vertex count or a label count."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "If the Mesh also carries **face-based** scalar slots, the report states how many but does not analyse them (face data is rarely used directly for measurement reporting in Dragonfly)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "What the report contains",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "table",
     "table": {
      "headers": [
       "Part",
       "Content"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Overview",
        "The mesh's vertex and label counts, then **two separate blocks** (vertex-based / label-based), each a table with one row per measurement: mean, median, min, max, std, unit and sample count N — plus a short note stating how the families differ. A family with no data is explicitly reported as \"(none on this mesh)\"."
       ],
       [
        "One page per measurement",
        "A worksheet in xlsx, a slide in pptx; every sheet name / slide title is prefixed `Vertex-` or `Label-`"
       ],
       [
        "1. Histogram",
        "The distribution of that measurement (axis carries the unit, the title states which family it belongs to)"
       ],
       [
        "2. 3D screenshot",
        "A 3D rendering showing only this Mesh, coloured by that measurement"
       ],
       [
        "3. Animated GIF (optional)",
        "8 rotated views of the same measurement combined into a looping GIF. pptx embeds the GIF itself (**PowerPoint animates it in slideshow mode**); xlsx shows its first frame and states the GIF filename (both the GIF and the stills stay in the output folder)."
       ],
       [
        "4. Per-label values (label-based only)",
        "The complete list of label names and their values"
       ]
      ]
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "How to use it",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "steps",
     "steps": [
      "Make sure the target Mesh already carries measurements (compute them first with e.g. Compute Vertex Measurements / Compute Overall Measurements; the list tells you when a mesh has none).",
      "Open Prototype Apps ▸ Create a Report from Mesh… from the menu.",
      "Pick a **published** Mesh from the drop-down (the brackets show its vertex count, label count and how many vertex-based / label-based measurements it has); press \"↻ Refresh\" if you just created it.",
      "Choose the output format (Excel or PowerPoint) and whether to include 3D screenshots.",
      "For the rotating animation, tick \"Add animated GIF for each measurement (3D rotation)\" and adjust the number of angles (default 8) and the per-frame delay (default 250 ms) if you want.",
      "Set the output folder and press \"Generate report\". The output folder opens automatically when it finishes."
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "More angles and more measurements mean more capture time, since every angle is a separate render. 8 angles is a good clarity/time compromise; for a quick result leave the GIF unticked and keep just the single 3D screenshot."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "How it works (technical path)",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "**Reading measurements**: the vertex scalar slots and the label scalar slots are walked separately (`get<Vertex|Label>ScalarValuesSlotCount` / `…ScalarValues` / `…ScalarValueDescription` / `…ScalarValueDimensionUnit`), and the label name list is read too, so every label-based value can be attributed to a named label. On 2027.1 the first label slot is the label-ID column, not a measurement (Dragonfly's own panels skip it), so it is not reported."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "**Statistics and histograms**: numpy computes mean/median/min/max/std ignoring NaN; matplotlib's object-oriented Agg interface renders the histogram PNGs (never pyplot, to avoid fighting over global state inside the Dragonfly process)."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "**3D screenshots**: the current 3D view (or, from a 2D view, the first 3D view of its layout) is maximised for the capture - reversibly, the layout itself is never replaced. Volumes, ROIs, Multi-ROIs, meshes, graphs, rulers, boxes and planes are temporarily hidden so only this Mesh shows (annotations such as labels, arrows and points are left as they are). The mesh is coloured by each measurement in turn, and the colouring is read back: a measurement this Dragonfly build cannot colour by gets no screenshot rather than a picture of another measurement. `ReportHelper.captureSnapshot` takes the picture. Every object's 2D and 3D visibility, every view's camera and slab thickness, the mesh's colouring and the maximised view are recorded first and put back afterwards - also when a capture fails half way. When the layout has no 3D view the report asks before it starts, and then contains no screenshots; the statistics and histograms are still produced."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "**Animated GIF**: starting from the 3D view's current camera, N equally spaced camera poses are generated around the vertical axis with `Camera.getRotatedAroundAxis`, one frame is captured per pose, and Pillow writes the sequence as a `loop=0` GIF (frames are scaled down past a width cap so the pptx stays reasonably small)."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "**Writing the files**: xlsx via openpyxl (overview table plus one worksheet per measurement with the images embedded); pptx via python-pptx (title slide, one overview slide per family, one slide per measurement, with the GIF embedded as a real `.gif` so the animation survives)."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "**Threading**: reading objects and capturing 3D views must happen on the UI thread (a Dragonfly requirement); the statistics, histograms, GIF assembly and file writing then run on a worker thread, so the UI never freezes."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Share on the web (publish + link + QR code)",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "After a report is generated, the **Share the report on the web** group can turn the same report into **one self-contained HTML file** (every image and rotation GIF embedded) and upload it to free static hosting. The panel then shows the URL, copies it to the clipboard on one click, and shows a QR code so a phone can open it."
    },
    {
     "kind": "h3",
     "text": "Three visibility levels - understand the difference first"
    },
    {
     "kind": "table",
     "table": {
      "headers": [
       "Level",
       "Mechanism",
       "Who can see it"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Public",
        "a readable, fixed path; indexing allowed",
        "anyone; the link may show up in search results"
       ],
       [
        "Unlisted (default)",
        "a 22-character unguessable path + noindex,nofollow",
        "only people with the link. **This is not authentication** - once forwarded, the link cannot be recalled"
       ],
       [
        "Password protected",
        "the report body is AES-256-GCM encrypted and decrypted in the browser via WebCrypto",
        "anyone who has the link AND the password. The host, and anyone who finds the URL, holds ciphertext only"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "Free hosting generally does NOT offer real authentication (GitHub Pages for a private repository needs Pro/Team; password protection on Netlify/Vercel is a paid feature). Password mode is therefore the only way to get real confidentiality on a free tier. The password is NEVER stored anywhere - send it through a different channel than the link."
    },
    {
     "kind": "h3",
     "text": "How to use it"
    },
    {
     "kind": "steps",
     "steps": [
      "Generate the report as usual first (the share group stays disabled until a report exists).",
      "Pick the host: GitHub Pages / Netlify / Gitee Pages.",
      "Fill in that host's account fields (repository, token, ...). They are remembered for next time.",
      "Choose the visibility; for password mode, type the password to use this time.",
      "Press \"Publish to the web\". A confirmation dialog lists the filename, size, host, path and visibility - check it before confirming.",
      "Copy the URL, or let the other person scan the QR code. The QR image is also saved next to the report (report_qr.png) so you can drop it into a slide.",
      "Press \"Take it down\" when you no longer want it shared; the published file is deleted from the host."
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "h3",
     "text": "Token safety (important)"
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "The token is stored in PLAIN TEXT in the settings file under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\settings\\`. So use a **fine-grained PAT scoped to ONE dedicated repository** with Contents and Pages write access; do **not** use a classic `repo` token, which can rewrite every repository the account owns. Also remember that uploading a report publishes the image data inside it to a third-party server."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "Per-host notes: **GitHub Pages** on a free account needs a PUBLIC repository and takes 10-60 s to build after each publish, so a brief 404 right after publishing is normal. **Netlify** is live in seconds and its random subdomain is already hard to guess. **Gitee Pages** is fast inside China but does NOT rebuild automatically on the free tier - after uploading you must click 更新 once on the repository's Pages page, and Gitee requires real-name verification before Pages works at all."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "Every publish is recorded in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\published_reports.json` (time, host, URL, visibility, size) so you can see what of yours is online."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Share Report tab - a temporary link that dies with the window",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "The second tab, **Share Report**, is the opposite of the section above: no account, no upload, and no lasting trace. It opens a **Cloudflare quick tunnel** - one OUTBOUND connection from this machine, so no firewall rule and no inbound access are needed - and Cloudflare hands back a random public address such as `https://green-pond-fox-tree.trycloudflare.com`. The report is served from THIS computer while the window stays open. Close the window and the address stops working permanently: Cloudflare never reissues it."
    },
    {
     "kind": "h3",
     "text": "Which of the two sharing routes to use"
    },
    {
     "kind": "table",
     "table": {
      "headers": [
       "",
       "Share the report on the web (first tab)",
       "Share Report (this tab)"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Lifetime",
        "until you take it down",
        "only while this window is open"
       ],
       [
        "Needs an account",
        "yes (GitHub / Netlify / Gitee)",
        "no"
       ],
       [
        "Report is uploaded",
        "yes, to a third-party server",
        "no - served from this machine"
       ],
       [
        "Confidentiality",
        "unlisted path, or a real password (AES-256-GCM)",
        "none: anyone with the link can read it"
       ],
       [
        "Reliability",
        "a normal hosting service",
        "a Cloudflare TEST facility: rate-limited, no availability promise"
       ],
       [
        "Use it for",
        "delivering results to a customer",
        "letting somebody outside look at this for the next ten minutes"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "There is deliberately NO password on this route. While sharing, anyone who has the link can read the report, and a link can be forwarded or logged. The protection is that the window closes. Because of that, this tab must not be used as a delivery channel for customer results - use the first tab for that."
    },
    {
     "kind": "h3",
     "text": "How to use it"
    },
    {
     "kind": "steps",
     "steps": [
      "Generate the report on the first tab (the Start button stays disabled until a report exists, and is disabled again while a new report is being generated).",
      "Switch to Share Report and press \"Start sharing\".",
      "The first time only, it offers to download Cloudflare's cloudflared.exe (about 52 MB) and says where from. It is saved once per user under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\cloudflared and reused afterwards.",
      "A confirmation dialog states the file, its size, that there is no password, and what ends the share. Read it: the report contains your image data.",
      "Wait. The status goes \"Address issued - waiting for it to start answering. Do not send it yet.\" Nothing is offered for copying during this stage on purpose.",
      "Once the status turns green, use \"Copy link\", or let the other person scan the QR code (also saved next to the report as share_qr.png).",
      "\"Preview locally\" opens the same page from this machine in your own browser - it is byte for byte what the recipient sees, served by the same server.",
      "\"Opened N times; last at ...\" counts real reads of the page, so you can tell whether the other person actually looked.",
      "Press \"Stop sharing\" when done - or simply close the window, or quit Dragonfly."
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "h3",
     "text": "The three outcomes, and why there are three"
    },
    {
     "kind": "table",
     "table": {
      "headers": [
       "Status",
       "What it means",
       "What to do"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "Live (green)",
        "the public address was fetched from this machine and served our own page, so it is definitely routable",
        "send the link"
       ],
       [
        "Could not confirm (amber)",
        "the tunnel is open, but this machine could not reach the address within 2 minutes - usually its DNS name has not started resolving here yet, or Cloudflare rate-limited a repeated quick tunnel",
        "the link is still offered: the recipient's DNS is not this one, so send it and ask, or stop and try again"
       ],
       [
        "Sharing failed (red)",
        "cloudflared never reported an address at all - normally no outbound internet access, or a proxy blocking it",
        "check outbound access from this machine; the log has cloudflared's own output"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "MEASURED on 2026-08-07 from this network, and the reason the amber state exists: of three consecutive quick tunnels, ONE became reachable (after 45 seconds) and two never resolved at all. The address is issued about 40 seconds before it starts working, so a tab that handed it over immediately would have you send a link that answers 404 - which is why this one waits, and says which of the three cases it is in rather than claiming success or failure it cannot prove."
    },
    {
     "kind": "h3",
     "text": "What is exposed, and what is not"
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "Only the one report page is served, from memory - never a directory, and never a file path a request could influence. `noindex` is sent both as a header and as a meta tag, and `robots.txt` disallows everything, so a crawler that stumbles on the address does not carry the report into a search index that would outlive the share. Anything that could write (POST, PUT, DELETE) is refused. The served page carries a bilingual banner telling the reader the link is temporary."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "If Dragonfly is killed without closing the share, the tunnel process can outlive it and keep the address registered. Every tunnel is therefore recorded, and the tab sweeps leftovers from a previous session when it opens - so \"closing the window kills the link\" also holds after a crash."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Requirements",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "No venv, GPU or admin rights — pure in-process. xlsx, histograms and GIFs use Dragonfly's own openpyxl, matplotlib and Pillow respectively; choosing pptx downloads python-pptx once on first use (offline afterwards). 3D screenshots and GIFs need a real 3D view; with no usable view the captures are skipped and the statistics and histograms are still produced."
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}

{
  "title": "Photo2Mesh: Meshroom",
  "cn": {
    "func": "Photo2Mesh: Meshroom 使用经典的 Meshroom/AliceVision 摄影测量流程，从一组普通照片重建真实物体的三维表面网格。您可以选择一个照片文件夹，或选择已加载到 Dragonfly 中的图像堆栈，点击\"重建网格\"（Reconstruct Mesh），即可生成带纹理的表面网格并直接发布到 Dragonfly 场景中。注意：重建结果的尺度和朝向是任意的，需要时请自行缩放；不透明、纹理丰富且照片重叠度高的物体重建效果最佳。",
    "scenarios": "适用于无需 CT 扫描即可获得实物三维数字模型的场合——例如数字化地质或生物标本、存档工业零件或失效样品、将物体外表面与其 CT 扫描的内部结构进行对比，或为可视化、教学和演示制作网格模型。",
    "requirements": "首次使用需通过面板中的安装按钮下载独立的 Meshroom 软件包（约 2 GB）；需要联网和一块 NVIDIA CUDA 显卡。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Photo2Mesh: Meshroom reconstructs a 3D surface mesh of a real object from a series of ordinary photographs, using the classic Meshroom/AliceVision photogrammetry pipeline. Select either a folder of photos or an image stack already loaded in Dragonfly, click Reconstruct Mesh, and a textured surface mesh is created and published directly into your Dragonfly scene. Note that the result has arbitrary scale and orientation, so rescale it as needed; opaque, well-textured objects with good photo overlap give the best results.",
    "scenarios": "Useful whenever you need a 3D digital model of a physical sample without a CT scan — e.g. digitizing geological or biological specimens, archiving industrial parts or failure samples, comparing an object's outer surface with its CT-scanned interior, or creating meshes for visualization, teaching, and presentations.",
    "requirements": "First use downloads the self-contained Meshroom package (~2 GB) via the panel's setup button; needs internet and an NVIDIA CUDA GPU."
  }
}

{
  "title": "Mesh to CAD (STEP)",
  "cn": {
    "func": "把场景中的 mesh 转换成 CAD 实体/壳体并导出 STEP / IGES / BREP。选择 mesh、后端、输出格式与输出文件后点击 Convert：结果写出 CAD 文件（水密网格→实体，否则为壳），并给出“打开输出文件夹”。后端：OpenCASCADE 逐面实体（精确、每个三角面一张面，缝合成壳/实体，宽松 LGPL，始终可用）、OpenCASCADE 凸包实体（简化）、以及 Point2CAD 自动参数化 B-rep（实验性）。",
    "scenarios": "适用于把 CT/照片重建/分割得到的 mesh 送入 CAD 流程：导出 STEP 后在 FreeCAD/SolidWorks 等中打开、做计量或装配参考；逆向工程与存档。需要“干净参数化 CAD”时可试 Point2CAD（见环境需求的许可提示）。",
    "requirements": "首次使用点击 Setup Environment：在短路径 %LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\MeshToCAD\\venv 建 venv（OpenCASCADE 的 DLL 若装在很深的目录会触发 Windows MAX_PATH 而加载失败），联网安装 cadquery-ocp/trimesh/numpy/scipy。OpenCASCADE 后端无需 GPU。⚠ Point2CAD 后端为 CC-BY-NC 4.0，仅限非商业/研究用途，且为实验性：需要自备 GPU 环境（设 MESH2CAD_P2C_DIR / MESH2CAD_P2C_PY）与已分割的点云，尚待实机验证；商业/稳健用途请用 OpenCASCADE 后端。在 Dragonfly 2027.1（Python 3.14）上不能用 Dragonfly 自带的 Python 创建该环境：请安装 python.org 的 Python（3.12、3.13 或 3.14）并填入面板的 Base Python；Dragonfly 2025.1 无需另装 Python。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Converts a scene mesh into a CAD solid/shell and writes STEP / IGES / BREP. Pick a mesh, a backend, an output format and an output file, then click Convert: the CAD file is written (watertight mesh -> solid, else shell) with an 'Open output folder' button. Backends: OpenCASCADE faceted solid (exact - each triangle a face, sewn into a shell/solid; permissive LGPL, always available), OpenCASCADE convex-hull solid (simplified), and Point2CAD automatic parametric B-rep (experimental).",
    "scenarios": "Bring meshes from CT / photogrammetry / segmentation into a CAD workflow: export STEP to open in FreeCAD/SolidWorks etc., for metrology or assembly reference; reverse engineering and archival. For clean parametric CAD, try Point2CAD (see the license note in Requirements).",
    "requirements": "First use: click Setup Environment - it builds a venv at the SHORT path %LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\MeshToCAD\\venv (OpenCASCADE DLLs hit the Windows MAX_PATH limit if installed under a deep folder) and pip-installs cadquery-ocp/trimesh/numpy/scipy. The OpenCASCADE backends need no GPU. WARNING: the Point2CAD backend is CC-BY-NC 4.0 (NON-COMMERCIAL / research only) and experimental - it needs your own GPU environment (set MESH2CAD_P2C_DIR / MESH2CAD_P2C_PY) and a segmented point cloud, and is live-verify pending; use an OpenCASCADE backend for commercial/robust use. On Dragonfly 2027.1 (Python 3.14) the bundled Python cannot build this environment: install a python.org Python (3.12, 3.13 or 3.14) and enter it in the panel's Base Python field; Dragonfly 2025.1 needs no separate Python."
  }
}

{
  "title": "Microstructure Statistics & RVE",
  "cn": {
    "func": "测量多相微观结构，并回答单一视场自己回答不了的问题：样品要取多大，测出来的数才算稳住了。两项核心能力是三相界线与代表体元收敛。三相界线定义在三相交汇的体素棱上，按各方向自己的间距换算长度，同时给出原始值与 2/3 格点计数修正值，并把总长度与有效长度分开——有效部分只统计三个相各自都能通过自身相连到外界的那一段，这才是电化学上真正可用的长度。代表体元一侧扫描子体尺寸，拟合离散度随盒体积的下降规律，给出代表尺寸 L*，并明确标注它是在实测尺寸范围内内插得到、由拟合幂律外推得到，还是根本没有达到。体积分数、界面面积（同时给出阶梯与移动立方体两种口径及其比值）、连通性与弦长作为支撑列一并给出。六个编号步骤：选择 MultiROI 标签或通道阈值，并为三个标签指定 A、B、C 角色；设定体素尺寸、裁剪范围与标签 0 的含义；勾选指标与采样设置；在抽稀副本上预览（不发布任何对象）；全分辨率运行；最后导出表格、单文件 HTML 报告与 JSON，或发布代表体元盒、最大贯通连通域与有效三相线掩膜。 另有一个不编号的「分享报告」标签页：通过 Cloudflare 临时快速隧道，把已生成的单文件 HTML 报告直接从本机发布出去，不上传、也不需要账号；该地址是公开的、没有密码（拿到链接的任何人都能读，而页面本身就带着您的图像数据），隧道建立后大约还要 40 秒它才开始响应，停止分享或关闭窗口后即永久失效。",
    "scenarios": "适用于：测量电池与燃料电池电极的三相界线，并看清其中有多少是真正可用的；判断多孔介质或复合材料研究需要多大的视场，数值才不再漂移；以一种别人能够比对的口径报告界面面积；为一幅图像上测得的数值附上一份诚实的不确定度说明。",
    "requirements": "无需任何安装或配置：完全运行在 Dragonfly 自带的 Python 中，使用其已附带的 NumPy 与 SciPy。不联网、不下载、不需要 GPU、不创建虚拟环境。 唯一的例外是可选的「分享报告」标签页：它需要本机能访问外网，并会在您确认后一次性下载 Cloudflare 的 cloudflared.exe（约 52 MB，按用户保存）。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Measures a multi-phase microstructure and answers the question one field of view never answers on its own: how large a volume do you need before the numbers stop moving? The two headline quantities are the triple phase boundary and RVE convergence. The triple phase boundary lives on the voxel edges where three phases meet, each direction scaled by its own spacing, reported both raw and with the 2/3 lattice-count correction, and split into the total and the active part whose three phases each reach the outside through their own phase — the only length that is electrochemically usable. The convergence side sweeps subvolume size, fits how the scatter falls with box volume, and reports the representative size L* together with whether it was interpolated inside the sizes actually measured, extrapolated from the fitted power law, or never reached at all. Volume fraction, interfacial area (in both the staircase and marching-cubes conventions, with their ratio), connectivity and chord lengths come along as supporting columns. Six numbered steps: choose MultiROI labels or a Channel threshold and give three labels the roles A, B and C; set the voxel size, the crop and what label 0 means; choose the metrics and the sampling; preview on a decimated copy, publishing nothing; run at full resolution; and finally export the tables, the self-contained HTML report and the JSON, or publish the representative box, the largest percolating component or the active triple-line mask. An unnumbered Share Report tab puts the finished one-file HTML report on the internet temporarily through a Cloudflare quick tunnel, served straight from this machine with no upload and no account: the address is public and has no password (anyone with the link can read it, and the page carries your image data itself), it only starts answering roughly 40 s AFTER the tunnel is started, and it stops working permanently once sharing is stopped or the window is closed.",
    "scenarios": "Use it to measure the triple phase boundary of a battery or fuel-cell electrode and see how much of it is actually usable; to decide how large a field of view a porous-media or composite study needs before its numbers are stable; to report an interfacial area in a convention someone else can compare against; and to attach an honest uncertainty statement to a number measured on a single image.",
    "requirements": "Nothing to install or configure: it runs entirely in Dragonfly's own Python using the NumPy and SciPy it already ships. No internet, no download, no GPU, no virtual environment. The one exception is the optional Share Report tab: it needs outbound internet access and downloads Cloudflare's cloudflared.exe once (about 52 MB, kept per user) with your confirmation."
  }
}

