  "en": {
    "func": "What it does: treat one PUBLISHED MultiROI or ROI as the ground truth and another as the segmentation to evaluate, then quantify how well they agree in TRUE 3D. Two levels of result: VOXEL level TP/FP/FN with precision, recall, Jaccard (IoU) and F1 (Dice); and OBJECT level, where objects are first matched one-to-one by IoU and then counted as TP/FP/FN across a sweep of IoU thresholds (0.50 to 0.95 in steps of 0.05 by default, the COCO/StarDist convention), giving AP = TP/(TP+FP+FN), mAP and panoptic quality. Optional boundary agreement (ASSD and 95% Hausdorff, converted to world units using the voxel spacing). Results export to Excel/CSV/JSON plus plots, and the TP/FP/FN overlay can be published as a new MultiROI (green = agree, red = extra, blue = missed) so the errors are visible directly in the 2D/3D views.\n\nThis ports the metric set of the ImageJ plugin MiC (MIT, https://github.com/MultimodalImagingCenter/MiC). MiC's README says it \"works only in 2D for now, each slice will be compared to corresponding slice\", but that line is stale relative to its own code: MiC ships a registered \"Mask instant Comparator 3D\" entry, so 3D is NOT what sets this plugin apart. What it actually offers is running inside Dragonfly on published MultiROI/ROI objects, with the results wired into Dragonfly's own tables, overlays and reports, plus boundary distances (ASSD / Hausdorff) in world units, which MiC does not compute. The per-slice mode is kept so results can be compared with the ImageJ original.\n\nEvery tab can be opened and edited at any time, and no tab is ever locked shut. The Export button in particular is no longer greyed out before a comparison exists: it stays live and, when pressed, says that there is nothing to export yet and which step produces it - a disabled button cannot tell you why it is disabled. There is deliberately no 'Execute All Steps' button here, because there is only one compute step: the Compare button already does read, measure and optionally publish in a single press, the results tab is a passive view, and the only other action writes files to a folder you must choose.",
    "scenarios": "Typical scenarios: scoring a deep-learning segmentation (Cellpose, nnU-Net, Dragonfly's own DL segmentation) against a hand-drawn reference; measuring inter-annotator agreement; deciding objectively whether a parameter change helped; reporting standard numbers such as Dice and mAP in a paper; and most practically, seeing WHERE the false positives and false negatives are, which distinguishes a global offset from missed small objects from neighbours merged into one.\n\nNote: mAP is only meaningful when BOTH inputs are labelled MultiROIs. With a binary ROI the whole foreground counts as a single object, so only the voxel-level metrics apply.",
    "requirements": "Requirements: no GPU, no internet, no admin rights and nothing to install. Everything runs inside Dragonfly on the bundled numpy. Object matching uses the optimal assignment (Hungarian) when scipy is available and falls back to greedy otherwise (identical results at IoU >= 0.5). Boundary distances need scipy; Excel export needs openpyxl; plots need matplotlib - all shipped with Dragonfly. The two inputs must come from the SAME image grid (identical shape); a mismatch is reported as an explicit error rather than silently producing wrong numbers."
