          "text": "**Grain volume barely changes, and is arguably better.** The measured table already multiplies every voxel by the FULL slice step - 0.5 um on the reference stack - so it already extrudes each section across the whole gap and puts a vertical wall at the section. Interpolation does not add volume; it replaces that extrusion with a boundary that moves across the gap. The specimen's total volume is preserved to within the half-step the stack gives up at its top and its bottom face, where there was no second section to interpolate against: the interpolated total measures `((Z - 1) + 1/factor) / Z` of the measured one - 97.8% on a 40-section stack at 8x, 91.3% on a 10-section one. What shifts between two neighbouring grains is only the part of the gap the two treatments disagree about."
