    "func": "The second Hands-on Project in the 'Hands-on Workshops' category: a four-tab teaching wizard covering the whole chain from an image full of particles to each particle's size to the size distribution. Four tabs: (1) Concepts — semantic vs instance segmentation (why separating particles from background is not enough; you must separate each particle from its neighbours; ROI vs MultiROI); (2) Instance-segmentation methods — classic watershed, SNOW, Fast-split (semantic-then-split) and Cellpose-SAM (deep learning, instances directly from the grayscale image with no semantic step), with a comparison; (3) MultiROI measurements — what can be measured per label (volume, surface area, equivalent diameter, Feret, sphericity…), units and pitfalls; (4) Particle size definitions & computation — the many definitions (equivalent-volume, projected-area, Feret, Martin, sieve, Stokes diameters…) and which Dragonfly can compute directly per particle. Every tab has many '📖 Learn' buttons (resizable, 中文|EN-switchable teaching pop-ups) and '🛠 Try it' buttons: create a synthetic touching-particles sample, Otsu semantic ROI, an in-process distance-transform watershed instance segmentation → published MultiROI, per-particle measurement (volume / equivalent diameter / Feret bounding box), and a size distribution (number- and volume-weighted D10/D50/D90 + span); heavier methods (SNOW, Cellpose-SAM) point to the matching Prototype Apps. The core actions run fully in-process on Dragonfly's bundled numpy/scipy/skimage — no venv, no download, no internet.\n\n**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the first tab runs the whole workshop unattended - pick the image, threshold it, separate the touching particles by watershed, measure them, build the size distribution - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. It does not replace the hand-driven path: every per-step button and all the Learn material stay as they are; this is for the second pass, re-running everything after changing one parameter. Two tick boxes: generating a synthetic particle image is OFF by default, because it publishes a Channel and re-points the image picker at it, so an unattended run must not silently replace the image you chose; the semantic threshold is ON, because although it publishes an ROI, the merged blob it produces is the teaching point of Step 1. Every tab stays open at all times so the parameters can be read and changed BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses at its own button and says which step is missing, instead of the tab being locked shut.",
