  "en": [
    {"heading": "Introduction", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "This plugin answers a question that sounds simple and is easy to get wrong: **how big is this structure, really?** It erodes (or dilates) a segmented ROI or MultiROI step by step and records how many connected components survive each step. When a population of similarly sized structures disappears together, the component count drops sharply — and that step corresponds to their characteristic scale."},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "The panel has 6 numbered steps. Changing anything upstream invalidates every downstream result, so an exported number always matches what is on screen; the analysis runs on a worker thread, and cancel or failure publishes nothing."},
      {"kind": "note", "text": "**The reported radius comes from the Euclidean distance transform, not from counting erosion steps.** A step count can only resolve the scale to half a voxel, and the sign of that error depends on where the true radius falls between two integers: measured against phantoms, spheres came out systematically half a voxel too large and slabs half a voxel too small. The distance transform has no such bias and gives an exact physical length for any voxel aspect ratio. The erosion-step estimate is still reported separately as **Step estimate (±½ voxel)** for comparison."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Use cases", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Porous materials: pore size and pore-wall thickness."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Fibres, vessels, trabeculae: diameter and the spacing between them."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Coatings, membranes, vessel walls: thickness (use the thickness mode)."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "Grain packings: the characteristic grain size."},
      {"kind": "bullet", "text": "When the structure is interconnected and cannot be split into countable objects, a morphological scale is one of the few measurements still available."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Installation & enabling", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "steps", "steps": [
        "Install the Prototype Apps package (or tick this plugin in the App Store).",
        "This plugin is **disabled by default** — enable it in the App Store or the Menu Item Manager.",
        "**Restart Dragonfly completely** (plugins are discovered at startup only).",
        "Open it from: Prototype Apps ▸ Structure Scale Analyzer… (group: Measurements & Analysis)."
      ]}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Runtime environment & first-run setup", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "**Nothing to install or configure.** It runs entirely in Dragonfly's own Python using the NumPy and SciPy it already ships. No internet, no download, no GPU, no virtual environment."},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "Step 1 shows what this Dragonfly build supports: whether an ROI can be created from an array, whether a Channel can be published, whether MultiROI labels are readable. Unavailable actions are disabled up front rather than failing after a long run."}
    ]},
    {"heading": "Interface", "blocks": [
      {"kind": "p", "text": "The header always shows the environment, the current input, the running task, a progress bar and **Cancel**."},
      {"kind": "h3", "text": "1. Setup"},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "A short description of the method plus the capability probe."},
      {"kind": "h3", "text": "2. Input & Analysis Mode"},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "Pick an ROI mask or a MultiROI (**published objects only**). A MultiROI takes precedence and unlocks the per-label batch in step 6. Three modes: **structure scale** and **thickness** both erode (the first looks at the overall characteristic scale, the second at local half-thickness); **spacing between objects** dilates instead, measuring the scale on the background side. Connectivity is 6-connected (faces) or 26-connected (faces + edges + corners)."},
      {"kind": "h3", "text": "3. Parameters & Physical Scale"},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "The voxel size is read from Dragonfly's geometry (in millimetres); tick **Override voxel size** to set it by hand for objects without geometry. **Resample anisotropic data to isotropic** matters because otherwise one erosion step means a different physical distance along each axis. The iteration limit bounds the curve; smoothing sigma and the peak offset only affect the erosion-step estimate and **not** the reported radius."},
      {"kind": "h3", "text": "4. Preview & Run"},
      {"kind": "p", "text": "**Load mask** reads the data from Dragonfly and reports its shape and foreground voxel count, **Run analysis** runs the main measurement, and **Run per-label batch** analyses every label of a MultiROI independently."},
      {"kind": "h3", "text": "5. Curve & Results"},
