  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Computes the fluid velocity at every point of an image from a pair of tracer-particle frames, then derives vorticity, divergence, strain rate and maximum shear rate from that field. The engine is multi-pass normalised FFT cross-correlation: the interrogation window shrinks pass by pass (64/32 → 32/16 → 16/8 by default), each pass takes the previous field as a predictor and deforms both windows by half of it, and only the residual is measured. The correlation value is a true coefficient in [-1, 1] — two identical windows give exactly 1.0 at zero lag. Four sub-pixel peak estimators are offered and their peak-locking bias is **measured, not assumed**: at a particle-image diameter of 2 voxels the 2 x 3-point Gaussian locks by 0.0076 voxel against the parabola's 0.105, which is why the Gaussian is the default; at a diameter near 1 voxel all four fail (0.15 to 0.5 voxel), so the plugin warns instead of computing a field that would look fine. **Physical units come from you**: Δt in seconds and the pixel pitch in mm are entered in step 2, and the Run button stays disabled without both — without them there is a displacement and no velocity. Velocity is reported in mm/s, derived fields in 1/s, circulation in mm²/s. The vector spacing is the window **step** times the pixel pitch, never the pixel pitch itself, and a published field is placed at its first vector (the source origin plus half a window, then back half a node to that node's corner). Every derived field states its finite-difference scheme and that scheme's noise amplification: least-squares 5-point 0.447, central 1.000, Richardson 1.344, circulation contour 0.612 (= sqrt(3/8)). The lower-order least-squares scheme is the default because a real PIV field is noise-limited, not truncation-limited. Every vector carries its SNR (primary-to-second-peak ratio and peak-to-RMS ratio), the sub-pixel fit residual, the local neighbourhood dispersion and ten flag bits; **a replaced vector is an interpolation, not a measurement**, that flag travels into the CSV, the VTK file and the report, and any difference stencil touching one degrades to NaN and is counted rather than quietly becoming a one-sided difference. Six numbered steps: choose the pair source and the pairing; enter Δt and the pixel pitch, set the preprocessing and estimate the particle-image diameter; set the pass schedule, the correlation parameters, the vector validation and the difference scheme; preview one pair on a centred crop, publishing nothing; run at full frame, publishing nothing either; and finally publish u, v and speed, the vorticity / divergence / maximum shear rate / strain-rate magnitude, a Vector Field of glyphs or a streamline Graph, and export CSV, VTK and a self-contained HTML report. An unnumbered Share Report tab puts the finished one-file HTML report on the internet temporarily through a Cloudflare quick tunnel, served straight from this machine with no upload and no account: the address is public and has no password (anyone with the link can read it, and the page carries your image data itself), it only starts answering roughly 40 s AFTER the tunnel is started, and it stops working permanently once sharing is stopped or the window is closed.",
    "scenarios": "Use it for tracer-particle experiments in a water channel, a microfluidic device or a wind tunnel, where the answer is a velocity field plus vorticity and strain rate; to decide whether an acquisition's frame separation and seeding density were right at all (the preview's locking ratio and particle-image diameter answer exactly that); to publish the velocity field back into Dragonfly as Channels or a Vector Field so it can be viewed against the rest of the same acquisition; and to export a whole run with its provenance record as CSV / VTK / HTML for ParaView or for a paper.",
    "requirements": "Nothing to install or configure: it runs entirely in Dragonfly's own Python using the NumPy, SciPy, scikit-image (CLAHE only, imported lazily) and pyevtk (the VTK export only, imported lazily) it already ships. No internet, no download, no GPU, no virtual environment. The one exception is the optional Share Report tab: it needs outbound internet access and downloads Cloudflare's cloudflared.exe once (about 52 MB, kept per user) with your confirmation."
  }
}

