{
  "title": "Pores to Surface Analysis",
  "cn": {
    "func": "功能：从**一幅图像通道**出发，一条工作流走完「分割实体 → 自动标记内部空洞 → 转成 MultiROI → 测量每个空洞到零件表面的最小距离 → 按距离阈值筛查」。**第一步先提取实体**——对铸件而言就是金属部分，不含件外空气，也不含件内空洞：Otsu 或手动阈值（亮相/暗相/灰度区间），可选开闭运算，并默认「只保留最大的一块实体」（否则体数据中别处的一小块噪声会被当成独立零件，带来根本不在铸件里的空洞）。**第二步自动标记空洞**：对背景只做一次连通域标记，触及体数据边界的连通域是件外空气，其余每一个连通域就是一个被实体完全包围的空洞——空洞的分割结果与「外部」的判定出自同一次运算，不会自相矛盾；空洞连通性可选 26 或 6 连通。**第三步测距离**：把空洞表面的体素面与零件表面的体素面用 k-d 树匹配，得到从空洞表面到零件表面的最短距离，也就是空腔与外部之间最薄的一层材料。同时给出体素中心到体素中心的距离，**两种定义都会报告并给出实测差值**（两者约差一个体素，互为校核），不会只丢一个数字给你却不说它是哪种定义。零件被视场切断的地方，那条边界是**切面而不是零件表面**：插件会报告表面中有多大比例是切面，逐个空洞标出「最近表面是否落在切面上」，也可以把切面整体排除。**第四步发布**：空洞以 MultiROI 发布（每个空洞一个 label），并把到表面的距离、中心距离、等效直径、体积、切面标志作为**测量值写在对象上**，Dragonfly 自带的工具也能直接使用；实体与全部空洞也可另存为 ROI。**第五步分析**：设定距离阈值，表格中小于该阈值的空洞整行标红，可一键把已发布 MultiROI 中的同一批空洞也涂成红色，表格支持任意列排序并导出 CSV。除非点「发布」，本插件不会创建或修改会话中的任何对象。\n\n「数据源」页的**一键执行全部步骤（按当前设置）**会无人值守地跑完整个流程：读取图像 → 分割固相并找出孔隙 → 计算到表面的距离 → 发布，每一步都使用你按下按钮那一刻各自页签上显示的设置。其中只有「发布」会向会话写入对象，它被特意包含在内——否则无人值守跑完之后什么也不会留下。所有页签始终可以打开和修改参数，以便在运行**之前**逐页检查；某一步所需的输入尚不存在时，按下**它自己的按钮**会给出提示，说明缺的是哪一步。",
    "scenarios": "适用场景：铸件（压铸、砂铸、低压铸造）近表面气孔/缩孔的判废与分级——近表面孔在机加工后会露出成为漏点，通常按「到表面距离」而不是按孔径判定；增材制造件表层缺陷与壁厚裕度评估；焊缝与钎焊层内部气孔的埋深统计；任何需要「每个空洞离表面多远、有多少个低于某个距离」并要把结果交付成表格/CSV/带测量值的 MultiROI 的场合。",
    "requirements": "环境需求：无需安装、联网、GPU 或虚拟环境——进程内 PyQt6 + numpy + scipy（均为 Dragonfly 自带）。需要会话中有已发布的**单分量灰度图像通道**，且该数据已标定体素尺寸：**读不到体素尺寸时分析会被拒绝**（所有距离都将失去意义）。二维图像（单张切片）同样支持，此时不会把切片自身的上下两面误当成零件表面。计算量随体数据大小增长：分割与标记是整卷运算，距离测量只在表面采样点上进行，因此空洞多也不会显著变慢。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "What it does: one workflow over ONE image channel - segment the solid, mark the cavities it encloses, turn them into a MultiROI, measure every pore's minimum distance to the part surface, and screen them against a distance threshold. STEP 1, THE SOLID: for a casting that is the metal, not the air around the part and not the cavities inside it - an Otsu or manual threshold (bright phase, dark phase or a value band), optional opening/closing, and 'keep only the largest solid component' ON by default, because a speck of noise elsewhere in the volume is otherwise its own little part with its own cavities that are not in the casting at all. STEP 2, THE PORES, automatically: the background is labelled ONCE; the components that reach the volume border are the outside air and every other component is a cavity fully enclosed by the solid - so the pore segmentation and the decision about what counts as 'outside' come out of the same pass and cannot disagree. Pore connectivity is 26 or 6, your choice. STEP 3, THE DISTANCE: the voxel faces of each pore's surface are matched against the voxel faces of the part surface with a k-d tree, giving the shortest distance from the pore surface to the part surface - the thinnest wall of material between the cavity and the outside. The voxel-centre-to-voxel-centre distance is measured at the same time and BOTH are reported together with their measured difference (about one voxel apart, each a check on the other); no single number is handed over without saying which convention it is. Where the part runs out of the scanned volume, that boundary is a CUT, not a surface of the part: the run reports how much of the surface is such a cut, each pore reports whether its nearest surface point lies on one, and the cut can be excluded outright. STEP 4, PUBLISH: the pores go into the session as a MultiROI, one label per pore, carrying the distance to the surface, the centre distance, the equivalent diameter, the volume and the field-of-view flag as MEASUREMENTS on the object so Dragonfly's own tools can use them; the solid and all pores can also be published as ROIs. STEP 5, ANALYSIS: set a distance threshold and every pore below it is marked RED across its whole row, one click recolours the same pores red in the published MultiROI so the 3-D view and the table agree, the table sorts on any column, and everything exports to CSV. Nothing in the session is created or modified unless you press Publish.\n\n**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the Source tab runs the whole workflow unattended - read the image, segment the solid and find the pores, measure the distance to the surface, publish - each step with whatever its own tab shows at the moment you press it. Publishing is the only step that writes to the session, and it is included on purpose: without it an unattended run would leave nothing behind. Every tab stays open at all times so the parameters can be read and changed BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses when you press ITS button and says which step is missing.",
    "scenarios": "Typical scenarios: accepting or rejecting near-surface gas and shrinkage porosity in castings (die, sand, low-pressure) - a pore close to the surface breaks through when the part is machined and becomes a leak path, so the criterion is the distance to the surface rather than the pore diameter; sub-surface defect and wall-thickness margin assessment on additively manufactured parts; burial-depth statistics for porosity in welds and brazed joints; any case where the question is 'how far is each pore from the surface, and how many are below X' and the answer has to leave as a table, a CSV and a MultiROI carrying the measurements.",
    "requirements": "Requirements: no install, no internet, no GPU, no venv - in-process PyQt6 + numpy + scipy, all bundled with Dragonfly. Needs a published SINGLE-COMPONENT grey image channel whose voxel size is calibrated: the analysis is REFUSED when the voxel size cannot be read, because every distance would be meaningless. A 2-D image (a single slice) is supported and its own two Z faces are NOT mistaken for surfaces of the part. Cost grows with the volume: segmentation and labelling are whole-volume operations, while the distance measurement runs only on the surface sample points, so a large number of pores does not slow it down noticeably."
  }
}

{
  "title_en": "Pores to Surface Analysis",
  "title_cn": "Pores to Surface Analysis(空洞到表面距离分析)",
  "menu_en": "Pores to Surface Analysis...",
  "version": "1.0",
  "date": "2026-09-01",
  "en": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "One workflow over **one image channel**: segment the solid, mark the cavities it encloses, turn them into a MultiROI, measure how far every pore is from the surface of the part, and screen the pores against a distance threshold."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The five tabs **are** the workflow, and each one opens only once the step before it has produced something: **1 Source → 2 Segment → 3 Distance → 4 Publish → 5 Analysis**."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**Nothing in the session is created or modified until you press Publish.** Steps 2, 3 and 5 read the channel and compute; you can explore a threshold all day without touching the data."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the Source tab runs the whole workflow unattended - read the image, segment the solid and find the pores, measure the distance to the surface, publish - each step with whatever its own tab shows at the moment you press it. Publishing is the only step that writes to the session, and it is included on purpose: without it an unattended run would leave nothing behind. Every tab stays open at all times so the parameters can be read and changed BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses when you press ITS button and says which step is missing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "What counts as solid, outside and a pore",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Solid** is the material — for a casting, the metal. It is a threshold on the channel, optionally cleaned, and by default reduced to its largest connected component."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Outside** is every background voxel whose background component reaches the border of the volume. That is the air around the part."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**A pore** is any other background component: one that never reaches the border, i.e. a cavity fully enclosed by the solid. The background is labelled once and both answers come out of that same pass, so the pore segmentation and the decision about what counts as outside cannot disagree with each other."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**The part** is the solid plus its pores, and the boundary of the part is the **part surface** every distance below is measured to."
        },
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "**Keep only the largest solid component is ON by default, and that matters.** A speck of noise elsewhere in the volume is otherwise treated as its own little part, with its own cavities and its own surface — and those cavities are not in the casting at all."
        }
