    {
      "heading": "FAQ & troubleshooting",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**It says hard-core packing jammed** — that is a report, not a fault. Random sequential addition saturates near a volume fraction of 0.38 for spheres and lower for elongated bodies, and the number reported is the fraction actually achieved. Lower the target, switch to the penetrable model, or raise the attempt budget."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**It says N bodies are wider than the non-periodic domain** — the part outside the grid cannot be drawn, so the structure holds a truncated body rather than the one requested. Enlarge the domain, shrink the body, or make that axis periodic. Note that the fibre length defaults to the longest domain edge, so on the defaults the fibres are meant to span the box."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**It warns that the clipped spectral energy is large** (above 0.02) — the requested correlation is not the correlation of any real field. Fit the admissible Debye model instead, shorten the radial window, or use a larger and cleaner exemplar. The number is reported rather than absorbed precisely so you can decide whether the run is worth believing."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**It refuses with \"the shared radial grid would hold only N bins\"** — the comparison bin is the coarser of the exemplar pixel and the output voxel, and the shortest physical axis of the output box cannot fill enough bins. Enlarge the output box along that axis, or supply an exemplar sampled at least as finely as the output."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**It says it auto-switched to the anisotropic mode** — that is correct behaviour: the exemplar's axial correlation lengths differ by more than the limit. After the switch the out-of-plane direction is still an assumption, and the axis it was matched to is stated in the result — a plausible-looking structure built on a broken assumption is worse than none."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**The mean error looks comfortable but the near-field error is large** — the near field (r of 1 to 3 voxels) is what tells you the threshold and the S2 inversion are right, and the specific surface is the small-r slope of S2 (Debye), so an interfacial area measured on this volume is biased. The two numbers are always reported separately so that far-field comfort cannot hide a near-field problem."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**It says periodicity along Z was ignored** — a rough surface and a fracture are both height fields and cannot be periodic through their own thickness; Y and X are unaffected."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**The tube network is connected as a graph but does not percolate in the voxels** — percolation is measured after rasterisation, and a throat thinner than one voxel simply disappears. Raise the throat radius or the resolution rather than trusting the graph."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**It refuses because the exemplar is constant** — a level cut cannot reproduce a structure that has none; the message carries the volume fraction it measured, so check the threshold method, the polarity and the crop."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Notes & known limitations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The whole volume is built in memory; the peak-memory figure in step 2 is an order-of-magnitude estimate, so use the preview to settle parameters on large grids. One grid may not exceed 2^30 voxels."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Publishing is always an explicit step-6 action; neither preview nor run publishes anything, and any change to an input or a parameter drops the previous result and disables the exports and the publish buttons immediately."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The preview is built over the same physical box with the same seed on a coarser grid, which for these analytic generators is the same structure sampled coarser — but **anything finer than the draft voxel is missing from it**, so its numbers must not be reported."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "The parametric mode needs no published object at all; the exemplar mode needs a slice out of a **published** Channel, and every picker lists published objects only."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**This plugin does not measure.** Volume fraction, interfacial area, the triple phase boundary, representative-size convergence and uncertainty belong to **Microstructure Statistics & RVE** — including on the volumes generated here."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**How this relates to Create Synthetic Images, plainly.** That plugin offers 26 generators built on PoreSpy, far more than this one; but it is voxel-unit only, publishes Channels only, is a single-page generate-and-publish panel, and **nothing in it is periodic**. None of these four families exists there either: PoreSpy 2.4.2 has no ellipsoid generator at all, its fibres are monodisperse straight cylinders with integer radii and free overlap, there is no tube **network** with a throat-radius distribution, and there is no rough surface. Use this plugin when you need physical millimetres, anisotropic voxels, per-axis periodicity, MultiROI output or exemplar-constrained synthesis; use that one when a quick voxel-unit 2-D or 3-D image is all you want."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "Exemplar synthesis is Gaussian random fields only. Simulated annealing and patch-based multipoint synthesis are not implemented and there are no hooks for them."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**S2 does not control connectivity.** Two structures can share S2 exactly and percolate completely differently, so what comes out is a statistically plausible structure and not the user's structure."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "One 2-D exemplar constrains its own plane only; the out-of-plane direction has to be chosen from five ways of matching it, and both the choice and the reason are recorded in the result and in a warning."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "One run writes one phase label (a fracture may give the upper wall a second one). A multi-phase volume means generating in several runs and compositing them yourself; publishing as a Channel can composite onto a matrix Channel on the same grid."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "A body larger than the periodic cell wraps onto itself — the pattern still tiles exactly, but beyond eight periods the tiling is truncated, and both cases are warned about."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "References",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Which parametric families are worth offering, and how a generated volume should be reported, were informed by NREL's MATBOX Microstructure Analysis Toolbox (BSD 2-Clause, Copyright (c) 2020, Alliance for Sustainable Energy, LLC): https://github.com/NREL/MATBOX_Microstructure_analysis_toolbox — **no source was copied, ported, translated or bundled**, and no MATLAB, MATLAB Runtime or Octave is required at runtime. The idea of multiscale exemplar reconstruction came from micro-maker (BSD 2-Clause): https://github.com/davidt0x/micro-maker, also reference only. PoreSpy (MIT) is prior art for voxel-based generators and for two-point-correlation measurement and is **referenced but not used** — its generators cannot express anisotropic voxel spacing or periodic wrapping, and both are requirements here."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Method citations (no code relationship): Quiblier, Adler, Roberts and Torquato (Gaussian-field level-cut reconstruction, the basis of the exemplar module); Debye (the small-r slope of S2 against specific surface); Perram & Wertheim 1985 (the ellipsoid contact function used for non-overlapping packing); Brown 1995 (self-affine fracture surfaces with mismatched wavelengths); Widom (random sequential adsorption and its jamming limit). **Yeong & Torquato** (simulated-annealing reconstruction) is cited once only, to record that v1 deliberately does not implement it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "No GPL or LGPL component is imported: `edt` (LGPLv3+) and `cc3d` (LGPL) are both present in Dragonfly's Python environment and are **never imported** — every distance transform uses `scipy.ndimage.distance_transform_edt` and every connected-component labelling uses `scipy.ndimage.label`, and a test scans the shipped source to keep it that way. At runtime only Dragonfly's own NumPy and SciPy are used, both BSD-3-Clause. The full statement is in `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` in the plugin folder."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "TauFactor: Tortuosity Factor & Effective Diffusivity",
  "cn": {
    "func": "对分割后的三维图像中选定的相计算迂曲度因子（tau）和有效扩散系数，使用 GPU 加速的 TauFactor 2 求解器。选择一个 ROI、MultiROI 类别或 Channel 作为导通相，并选择扩散方向（X/Y/Z）；插件在该相内求解稳态扩散，报告 tau、有效扩散系数、体积分数、表面积以及渗透（连通性）检查结果，并将稳态浓度场作为 Channel 发布回场景中。内置演示功能可生成仿体微结构，无需自己的数据即可试用。",
    "scenarios": "广泛应用于电池与燃料电池电极研究、地球科学和材料科学，用于量化通过显微 CT 或 FIB-SEM 成像的多孔介质的传输特性——例如孔隙网络或电解质相对离子或气体扩散的阻碍程度。适合已完成多孔或多相微结构分割、需要定量传输指标（而不仅是几何指标）的用户。",
    "requirements": "首次使用会构建独立的计算环境（下载 PyTorch，约 2.5 GB）；需要联网；NVIDIA GPU 可选（CPU 也可运行）。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Computes the tortuosity factor (tau) and effective diffusivity of a chosen phase in a segmented 3D image, using the GPU-accelerated TauFactor 2 solver. Select a ROI, MultiROI class, or Channel as the conducting phase and choose the flow direction(s) (X/Y/Z); the plugin solves steady-state diffusion through the phase and reports tau, effective diffusivity, volume fraction, surface area, and a percolation (connectivity) check, and publishes the steady-state concentration field back into the scene as a Channel. A built-in demo can generate phantom microstructures so you can try it without your own data.",
    "scenarios": "Widely used in battery and fuel-cell electrode research, geoscience, and materials science to quantify transport properties of porous media imaged by micro-CT or FIB-SEM — e.g., how much a pore network or electrolyte phase impedes ion or gas diffusion. Useful to anyone who has segmented a porous or multi-phase microstructure and needs quantitative transport metrics rather than just geometry.",
    "requirements": "First use builds a separate compute environment (downloads PyTorch, ~2.5 GB); needs internet; NVIDIA GPU optional (CPU also works)."
  }
}

{
  "title": "CT/TEM Reconstruction (ASTRA)",
  "cn": {
    "func": "直接在 Dragonfly 中把锥束 X 射线投影图像重建为三维体数据。选择投影图像堆栈（可附加平场/暗场校正图像），输入射线源-探测器几何参数，选择重建算法（FDK、SIRT3D 或 CGLS3D），GPU 加速的重建在后台运行，结果以新的图像 Channel 形式导入，并带有正确的体素间距。面板还内置新手演示：自动生成三维体模、模拟 X 射线投影并完成重建，无需任何数据即可体验完整流程。此外还提供 TEM（透射电镜）倾斜序列重建模式：对平行束投影先做对齐再重建，支持 GPU 与 CPU。",
    "scenarios": "适合拥有显微 CT 或工业 CT 原始投影数据、希望在同一软件中完成重建与分析的用户：材料科学、工业检测与计量、电子失效分析、地球科学，以及需要调试重建参数和迭代算法的实验室或同步辐射 CT 用户。",
    "requirements": "首次使用需运行附带的环境安装脚本，在 WSL2 中构建 GPU 计算环境（约几分钟，需联网）；要求 Windows 系统装有 WSL2 并配备 NVIDIA 显卡。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Reconstructs a 3D volume from cone-beam X-ray projection images directly inside Dragonfly. Select your projection stack (with optional flat/dark correction images), enter the source-detector geometry, choose an algorithm (FDK, SIRT3D, or CGLS3D), and the GPU-accelerated reconstruction runs in the background and returns the result as a new image Channel with correct voxel spacing. A built-in beginner demo generates a synthetic phantom, simulates X-ray projections, and reconstructs it, so you can try the whole workflow with no data. A TEM (transmission electron microscopy) tilt-series mode is also available: it aligns and then reconstructs parallel-beam projections, on GPU or CPU.",
    "scenarios": "For anyone with raw micro-CT or industrial CT projection data who wants to reconstruct and analyze volumes in one place: materials science, industrial inspection and metrology, electronics failure analysis, geoscience, and lab or synchrotron CT users experimenting with reconstruction parameters and iterative algorithms.",
    "requirements": "One-time setup via the provided Setup Environment script builds a GPU compute environment in WSL2 (several minutes, needs internet); requires Windows with WSL2 and an NVIDIA GPU."
  }
}

{
  "title": "TrackMate-like Object Tracker",
  "cn": {
    "func": "该插件把一组按时间顺序排列的 Dragonfly MultiROI 当作对象检测结果进行追踪。每一帧的每个非零标签视为一个对象，插件计算质心和体积，用线性分配算法按最大距离、gap closing 和可选体积变化惩罚把对象连接成 track，并生成 tracks.csv。结果也可以按帧发布回 Dragonfly：每帧一个 MultiROI，标签值为稳定的 track ID。",
    "scenarios": "适用于已经完成分割、现在需要追踪颗粒、细胞、孔隙、缺陷、材料对象或其他 2D/3D label 对象随时间/步骤移动的场景。它不是 Fiji TrackMate 的完整复制，但覆盖了 Dragonfly 中最常用的 MultiROI 对象级追踪流程。",
    "requirements": "需要至少两个已存在的 MultiROI，按时间顺序添加。首次使用需要在插件 Setup tab 中创建独立 venv 并安装 numpy、scipy；安装时需要联网。运行时不需要 GPU、Fiji、ImageJ 或 Java。在 Dragonfly 2027.1（Python 3.14）上不能用 Dragonfly 自带的 Python 创建该环境：请安装 python.org 的 Python（3.12、3.13 或 3.14）并填入面板的 Base Python；Dragonfly 2025.1 无需另装 Python。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Tracks objects across an ordered sequence of Dragonfly MultiROIs. Each nonzero label in each frame is treated as one detection; the plugin computes centroid and volume features, links detections with linear assignment using max distance, gap closing, and an optional volume-change penalty, then writes tracks.csv. It can also publish one MultiROI per frame where labels are stable track IDs.",
    "scenarios": "For already-segmented particles, cells, pores, defects, material objects, or other 2D/3D labeled objects that need object-level tracking across time or process steps. It is not a full Fiji TrackMate clone, but covers the most common Dragonfly MultiROI tracking workflow.",
    "requirements": "Requires at least two existing MultiROIs added in temporal order. First use builds an isolated plugin venv and installs numpy and scipy from PyPI; internet is needed for setup only. No GPU, Fiji, ImageJ, or Java is required. On Dragonfly 2027.1 (Python 3.14) the bundled Python cannot build this environment: install a python.org Python (3.12, 3.13 or 3.14) and enter it in the panel's Base Python field; Dragonfly 2025.1 needs no separate Python."
  }
}

{
  "title": "Reconstruct from ZEISS TXRM (MBIRJAX)",
  "cn": {
    "func": "直接从 ZEISS Xradia 的 .txrm 投影文件重建三维体积，无需任何其它数据源。插件在 Dragonfly 进程内解析 .txrm（OLE2 容器，使用内置的纯 Python olefile），显示并校验采集几何，然后在一个独立的 Python 3.11+ 环境中调用开源的 MBIRJAX 完成锥束重建（FDK 滤波反投影，或迭代 MBIR），最后按显式点击把体积发布为 Dragonfly 的图像 Channel，体素间距按米写入。几何全部来自文件本身：投影、平场参考图、逐投影角度、源/探测器距离、光学放大倍数、转轴中心（CenterShift）与逐投影漂移，都由 MBIRJAX 从 .txrm 中读取，无需手输任何参数。第 4 步会做一次几何自洽校验（PixelSize × 几何放大 × 光学放大 是否等于 CamPixelSize × binning），这是防止体素尺寸差出 4/20/40 倍的关键一环。插件还支持只用扫描的一部分投影来重建，用于模拟部分角度被遮挡、投影数据无效的场景：可按张数丢弃开头/结尾若干张、保留某个角度窗口、或排除任意多段角度区间，三者可叠加；还可以一次给出一组保留跨度（例如 300/240/180/150/120 度），一次运行产出多个体积以便画出退化曲线。\n\n「输入」页的**一键执行全部步骤（按当前设置）**会无人值守地跑完流程：检查文件 → 汇总所选子集与预计内存 → 重建 → 发布，每一步都使用你按下按钮那一刻各自页签上显示的设置。**发布是复选框且默认关闭**：它会把整个体数据读入 Dragonfly 进程，实测全分辨率重建时约 62 GB，因此必须由你明确选择而不能是默认行为。**环境安装完全不在流程内**——它要创建虚拟环境并从网络安装 mbirjax——因此当环境缺失时，重建步骤会直接拒绝并指明是这一步。所有页签始终可以打开，以便在运行**之前**核对几何与参数；某一步所需的输入尚不存在时，按下**它自己的按钮**会给出提示，说明缺的是哪一步。",
    "scenarios": "适用于拿到 ZEISS Xradia Versa 的原始投影数据、但希望用开源方法自行重建的场合：复现或替代厂商 Reconstructor 的结果、调整重建参数（滤波器、降采样、裁剪、背景与亮点校正）、对短扫描/有限角数据改用迭代 MBIR，或在没有厂商软件许可的机器上处理 .txrm。若同目录存在厂商自己的 _recon.txm，插件会指出它，可作为对照基准。",
    "requirements": "首次使用点击「搭建运行环境」。重要：MBIRJAX 要求 CPython 3.11 或更高，而 Dragonfly 自带的是 3.10，因此本插件无法用 Dragonfly 的 Python 搭建环境，需要机器上另外装有 64 位的 Python 3.11+（推荐 python.org 的 3.12）；搭建步骤会自动探测并在只找到 3.10 时给出明确提示。联网一次，安装 mbirjax 0.7.2 与 jax/jaxlib 0.10.1。不需要 GPU：JAX 的 CUDA 版本只发布 Linux 轮子，Windows 上重建在 CPU 上运行，因此大数据集建议先用降采样试跑。环境建在插件目录之外，一次搭建对所有 Dragonfly 版本生效，且不会被 App Store 更新清除。不支持 mosaic（拼接）扫描。在 Dragonfly 2027.1（Python 3.14）上不能用 Dragonfly 自带的 Python 创建该环境：请安装 python.org 的 Python（3.12、3.13 或 3.14）并填入面板的 Base Python；Dragonfly 2025.1 无需另装 Python。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "Reconstruct a 3-D volume directly from a ZEISS Xradia .txrm projection file, with no other data source needed. The plugin parses the .txrm in Dragonfly's own process (an OLE2 container, read with a bundled pure-Python olefile), shows and validates the acquisition geometry, then runs the open-source MBIRJAX cone-beam reconstruction (FDK filtered back-projection, or iterative MBIR) inside a separate Python 3.11+ environment, and finally publishes the volume as a Dragonfly image Channel on an explicit click, with voxel spacing written in metres. Every geometric quantity comes from the file itself: projections, the flat-field reference, per-projection angles, source/detector distances, the optical magnification, the centre of rotation (CenterShift) and the per-projection drift are all read out of the .txrm by MBIRJAX, so nothing has to be typed in. Step 4 runs a geometry closure check (does PixelSize x geometric magnification x optical magnification equal CamPixelSize x binning?), which is the guard that prevents a voxel size wrong by a factor of 4, 20 or 40. It can also reconstruct from only PART of a scan, to simulate angles that were blocked or whose data was invalid: drop a number of projections from each end, keep an angular window, or exclude any number of angular ranges - the three combine. A series of retained spans (e.g. 300/240/180/150/120 deg) reconstructs several volumes in one run so a degradation curve can be plotted.\n\n**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the Input tab runs the workflow unattended - inspect the file, report the subset and the memory it will need, reconstruct, publish - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. Publishing is a tick box and is OFF by default: it reads the whole volume into Dragonfly's process, which on a full-resolution run was measured at 62 GB, so it has to be a decision rather than a default. Setting up the environment is NOT part of the run at all - it builds a venv and installs mbirjax over the network - so the reconstruction step refuses and names it if the environment is missing. Every tab stays open so the geometry and parameters can be checked BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses at ITS button and says which step is missing.",
    "scenarios": "For anyone holding raw ZEISS Xradia Versa projections who wants an open-source reconstruction: reproducing or replacing the vendor Reconstructor result, tuning reconstruction parameters (filter, downsampling, cropping, background and zinger correction), switching to iterative MBIR for a short or limited-angle scan, or processing a .txrm on a machine without a vendor software licence. When the vendor's own _recon.txm sits next to the scan the plugin points it out, so it can be used as a reference.",
    "requirements": "First use: click Set up environment. IMPORTANT: MBIRJAX requires CPython 3.11 or newer while Dragonfly bundles 3.10, so this plugin cannot build its environment from Dragonfly's Python and needs a separate 64-bit Python 3.11+ installed on the machine (python.org 3.12 recommended); the setup step auto-detects one and refuses a 3.10 base with an explanation. Needs internet once, to install mbirjax 0.7.2 and jax/jaxlib 0.10.1. No GPU: JAX publishes CUDA wheels for Linux only, so on Windows the reconstruction runs on the CPU - try a downsampled run first on large datasets. The environment is built outside the plugin folder, so one setup serves every Dragonfly version and survives App Store updates. Mosaic (stitched) acquisitions are not supported. On Dragonfly 2027.1 (Python 3.14) the bundled Python cannot build this environment: install a python.org Python (3.12, 3.13 or 3.14) and enter it in the panel's Base Python field; Dragonfly 2025.1 needs no separate Python."
  }
}

{
  "title_en": "Reconstruct from ZEISS TXRM (MBIRJAX)",
  "title_cn": "Reconstruct from ZEISS TXRM (MBIRJAX)（从 ZEISS TXRM 重建）",
  "menu_en": "Reconstruct from ZEISS TXRM (MBIRJAX)...",
  "version": "1.0",
  "date": "2026-08-05",
  "en": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Reconstructs a 3-D volume straight from a **ZEISS Xradia .txrm projection file** using the open-source **MBIRJAX** reconstruction library (BSD-3-Clause, Purdue University). No other data source is required: the projections, the flat-field reference, the per-projection angles, the source/detector distances, the optical magnification, the centre of rotation and the per-projection drift are all read out of the .txrm itself."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "A .txrm is an OLE2 compound file — the same container as a .txm, but holding the PROJECTION set instead of a reconstructed volume. The plugin inspects it in Dragonfly's own process with a bundled pure-Python **olefile**, then hands the file to MBIRJAX in a separate Python environment for the reconstruction."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Two algorithms are offered. **FDK** is a filtered back-projection: fast, and the right default for a full 360-degree scan. **MBIR** is model-based iterative reconstruction: much slower, but better on short, limited-angle or sparse-view scans, where FDK applies no redundancy weighting and is only approximate."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**Nothing is published until you ask for it.** Inspecting and reconstructing create no Dragonfly objects. Only the *Publish volume as a Channel* button in step 6 creates anything, and it writes voxel spacing in **metres**, the ORS object-model convention."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Execute All Steps with Current Settings** on the Input tab runs the workflow unattended - inspect the file, report the subset and the memory it will need, reconstruct, publish - each step with whatever its own tab shows when you press it. Publishing is a tick box and is OFF by default: it reads the whole volume into Dragonfly's process, which on a full-resolution run was measured at 62 GB, so it has to be a decision rather than a default. Setting up the environment is NOT part of the run at all - it builds a venv and installs mbirjax over the network - so the reconstruction step refuses and names it if the environment is missing. Every tab stays open so the geometry and parameters can be checked BEFORE the run; a step whose input does not exist yet refuses at ITS button and says which step is missing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Use cases",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Reproducing or replacing the vendor Reconstructor result with an open-source pipeline whose every step is visible and adjustable."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Re-reconstructing an existing scan with different parameters — a different filter, a downsampled preview, cropped detector margins, background-offset or zinger correction — without going back to the instrument."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Reconstructing a short or limited-angle acquisition with iterative MBIR, which handles missing angular coverage far better than FDK."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Processing .txrm data on a machine that has no vendor software licence."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Cross-checking a reconstruction against the vendor's own: when a `*_recon.txm` sits in the same folder, the plugin names it in the QC report so it can be loaded (with the *Load TXM Files* plugin) and compared side by side."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Installation & enabling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "items": [
            "Open **Prototype Apps ▸ App Store**, find *Reconstruct from ZEISS TXRM (MBIRJAX)* in the **Reconstruction & Imaging** group, and enable it.",
            "Restart Dragonfly. Menu items are discovered at startup, so a restart is required — closing and reopening the window is not enough.",
            "The item appears as **Prototype Apps ▸ Reconstruct from ZEISS TXRM (MBIRJAX)...**",
            "Before the first reconstruction, complete the one-time environment setup described in the next section."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Runtime environment & first-run setup",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "**This plugin needs a Python that Dragonfly does not provide.** MBIRJAX requires **CPython 3.11 or newer**; Dragonfly bundles CPython 3.10. Every other plugin in this suite builds its environment from Dragonfly's own interpreter — this one cannot. You need a separate **64-bit Python 3.11+** installed on the machine, and **python.org 3.12** is the recommended choice."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "On the **2 Environment** tab, click **Set up environment**. The setup step ranks the interpreters it can find (python.org installs first, then the `py` launcher by version, then conda/miniforge, then PATH), refuses anything older than 3.11 or non-64-bit, and prints every interpreter it tried together with the reason each was rejected. If auto-detection picks the wrong one, type a path into **Base Python** and run it again."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Setup creates a virtual environment at `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\DragonflyPrototypeLabs\\TxrmMbirjax\\venv` and installs `mbirjax==0.7.2`, `jax==0.10.1` and `jaxlib==0.10.1` as wheels (internet needed once, roughly 200 MB). The location is deliberately OUTSIDE the plugin folder: one setup serves every installed Dragonfly version, it survives App Store updates, and it keeps the deep JAX package tree clear of the Windows 260-character path limit. The resolved interpreter is remembered in `txrm_mbirjax_config.json` and is never touched by *Reset to factory defaults*."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**CPU only on Windows.** JAX publishes CUDA builds for Linux only — there is no Windows CUDA jaxlib — so reconstruction runs on the CPU. Reconstruct a downsampled version first to judge timing before committing to a full-resolution run. Nothing is ever installed into Dragonfly's own `Python_env`."
        },
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "**Check the memory estimate before every full-resolution run.** Step 4 predicts it from the header: projections + a same-sized all-zero dark array that MBIRJAX allocates + the float32 sinogram + the volume, doubled for the peak. A measured full-resolution run of a 3401 x 1013 x 1013 dataset needed about 60 GB at peak; it reached 62 GB of page file and 258 million page faults, burned 19.3 CPU-hours in 64 minutes of wall clock and produced nothing. When the estimate exceeds half the available RAM the QC step says TOO BIG and names the exact factors to use instead. The reconstruction refuses outright only when the estimate exceeds the machine's total physical memory."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Interface",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Six numbered tabs, worked through in order."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**1 Input** — pick a `.txrm` file, or scan a folder to list every `.txrm` under it. The companion `*_Drift.txrm` (the drift-tracking scan, not reconstructable) is filtered out of folder scans automatically. **Inspect file** reads the geometry and moves you to the QC tab."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**2 Environment** — Base Python, *Set up environment*, *Check environment*, and the log that every step writes to (pip output, the runner command line, errors)."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**3 Parameters** — algorithm and filter, detector row/column downsampling, view subsampling, detector cropping, background-offset mode, zinger correction, auto-crop, and the length unit for 1 ALU."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**4 Preview & QC** — the geometry report: projection count and detector size, beam geometry and scanner type, SOD/ODD and the derived magnification, the voxel size the reconstruction must land on, the detector pitch, the centre shift, whether a flat field is present, the angular range, the closure check, the sibling vendor reconstruction, and any warnings or blocking problems."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**5 Run** — start and cancel, a progress bar and the current stage. Progress is read from the job's `status.json`; cancellation is a request the runner honours at its next checkpoint."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**6 Results & Export** — the full result record as JSON (shape, voxel size, spacing in metres, the geometry MBIRJAX actually used, library versions), a Channel title field, and the **Publish volume as a Channel** button."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**Cancel asks, then insists.** The first click requests cancellation, which the reconstruction honours at its next checkpoint; MBIRJAX offers no hook inside the reconstruction itself, so a **second** click stops the process outright. Closing the dock also stops it - it never keeps running invisibly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to use",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "steps",
          "items": [
            "**1 Input** — Browse to a `.txrm`, or scan a folder and pick one from the list. Click **Inspect file**.",
            "**4 Preview & QC** — read the report. `closure check : PASS` means the magnification chain is self-consistent. Any line beginning `BLOCKING` must be resolved before a run is possible; lines beginning `WARNING` are advisory (a limited-angle scan, a missing flat field, a varying source distance).",
            "**2 Environment** — if the status is not *ready*, click **Set up environment** and wait for `Setup complete.`",
            "**3 Parameters** — for a first pass set both downsample factors to 2 and *Keep every n-th projection* to 4. That reconstructs in a small fraction of the time and tells you whether the geometry and orientation are right before you spend the full run.",
            "**3 Parameters, Projection subset** — to reconstruct from only part of the scan, set *Drop first/last N projections*, or a *Keep angles* window, or *Exclude angle ranges*; the three combine. For a degradation study fill *Series: retained spans* with e.g. `300, 240, 180, 150, 120`. Click **Preview subset** and read the QC tab BEFORE running: it reports the kept count, the retained span, the largest angular hole and a warning per variant.",
            "**5 Run** — click **Run reconstruction**. The whole projection set is read inside the venv, so expect several minutes of I/O before the reconstruction itself starts, especially from a network drive.",
            "**6 Results & Export** — check `voxel_size_um` against the QC report's expected voxel size, set a Channel title, then click **Publish volume as a Channel**.",
            "Verify in Dragonfly: the new Channel's voxel size should equal the `.txrm` `PixelSize`. If the scan has a vendor `_recon.txm`, load it with *Load TXM Files* and compare a matching slice."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
