{
  "title": "Vessel Morphometry",
  "cn": {
    "func": "功能：从一条血管的封闭表面网格出发，沿其中心线逐个横截面测量管腔形态。插件先把网格内部体素化、骨架化，取骨架中最长的测地路径作为中心线，再在每个采样点上用**两种互相独立的方式**测量管腔，并计算曲线的微分几何量。报告始终**同时给出两个半径**：**内切球半径**（管腔内能放入的最大球，受横截面**短轴**限制）与**等效半径 sqrt(A/π)**（与横截面等面积的圆半径）。两者只在横截面为圆时才相等：参考流程给出的“半径”约为其 sqrt(A/π) 的 **54%**（即约 **1.9 倍**之差，相当于管腔被压扁约 3.5:1），而本插件所针对的两个标本实测比值为 **1.15 与 1.09**。报告始终同时给出这两个半径**及其比值**，所以本插件从不打印一个叫“半径”的数字 —— 请读比值，而不要沿用别处的数字。迂曲度**两种约定都给**：指数 L/D − 1 与比值 L/D（二者恰好相差 1，文献里同名混用；客户历史数据用的是**指数**）。**扭率为实验性指标**：它需要三阶导数，且在中心线局部接近直线处无定义，因此**一概不报告扭率最大值**（实测四种体素尺寸下最大值为 54.9 / 17.6 / 15.3 / 33.4 1/mm，而平均值变化在 8% 以内）——只报告平均值，并写出所用的拟合窗口。六步工作流：输入 / 标定与预处理 / 参数 / 预览与质检 / 运行 / 结果与导出，另有一个不编号的「分享报告」标签页。全过程不创建、不修改任何 Dragonfly 对象。",
    "scenarios": "适用场景：单根血管（离体或影像分割得到）的管腔粗细沿长度的分布；同一批标本左右侧、不同个体之间的对比；需要同时给出内切球半径与等效半径以解释“为什么两套历史数据对不上”的场合；需要把长度、迂曲度、曲率连同**实际使用的体素尺寸与拟合窗口**一起归档的场合（这些设置会改变结果，因此它们属于结果的一部分）。逐采样点 CSV 可直接进入 Excel 或 Python 做后续统计。",
    "requirements": "环境需求：**不需要虚拟环境、不需要 GPU、不需要管理员权限**，测量本身也不访问网络。唯一的例外是可选的「分享报告」标签页，它不做任何计算：发布报告会开启一条临时 Cloudflare 快速隧道，且首次使用会在你确认后下载 Cloudflare 的 cloudflared.exe（约 52 MB）。计算在 Dragonfly 进程内完成，使用 Dragonfly 自带的 numpy、scipy 与 scikit-image。还需要 trimesh：Dragonfly 2026.1 与 2027.1 自带它，2025.1 没有，因此本插件内置了一份（MIT、纯 Python），仅在 Dragonfly 本身缺失时使用。因此所有版本用的是同一个库，也仍然不需要下载任何东西。输入必须是已**发布**到会话中的 Mesh（在数据属性树里可见）。一根约 1200 个三角面的血管，在自动参数下大约需要 **20 秒**；测量在工作线程中运行，各阶段实时输出，「取消」在下一阶段边界处生效。**所有数值都以网格自身的单位表示，且任何环节都不做人工预缩放**：Dragonfly 的世界坐标以米为单位，顶点只在读取时一次性换算到工作单位（默认取 Dragonfly 自己的长度显示单位），此后长度为该单位、面积为单位²、体积为单位³、曲率与扭率为 1/单位。**不封闭（non-watertight）的网格没有体积**，报告会写「未定义」而不是给一个数字。"
  },
  "en": {
    "func": "What it does: measures the lumen of ONE vessel, cross-section by cross-section, along its own centerline, starting from a closed surface mesh. It voxelises the mesh interior, skeletonises it, takes the longest geodesic path through the skeleton as the centerline, and then at every sample measures the lumen TWO independent ways plus the curve's differential geometry. BOTH radii are always reported: the INSCRIBED SPHERE radius (the largest sphere that fits inside the lumen, so it is bounded by the SHORT axis of the cross-section) and the EQUIVALENT radius sqrt(A/pi) (the radius of a circle with the same cross-sectional area). They coincide only for a round section: the reference pipeline's own radius is about 54% of its sqrt(A/pi) - a factor of about 1.9, which is what a lumen flattened around 3.5:1 does to these two definitions - while the two specimens this plugin was built for measured 1.15 and 1.09. Both radii AND the ratio between them are always printed, which is why this plugin never prints a single number called \"the radius\": read the ratio for your own specimen instead of carrying a figure over from elsewhere. Tortuosity is given in BOTH conventions: the index L/D - 1 and the ratio L/D (they differ by exactly 1 and the literature uses both under the same name; the reference data set uses the INDEX). TORSION IS EXPERIMENTAL: it needs the third derivative and is undefined wherever the centerline is locally straight, so NO torsion MAXIMUM is reported at all (measured 54.9 / 17.6 / 15.3 / 33.4 1/mm across four voxel sizes while the mean stayed within 8%) - only the mean, with the fitting window that produced it stated. Six steps: Inputs / Calibration & Preprocessing / Parameters / Preview & QC / Run / Results & Export, plus an unnumbered Share Report tab. Nothing is ever created or modified in the session.",
    "scenarios": "Typical scenarios: how the calibre of a single vessel varies along its length; comparing left against right, or one specimen against another, across a batch; any case that has to explain why two historical data sets disagree, because the inscribed and the equivalent radius are both printed side by side with their ratio; and archiving length, tortuosity and curvature together with the VOXEL SIZE AND FITTING WINDOWS that produced them, since those settings change the answer and are therefore part of it. The per-sample CSV goes straight into Excel or Python for further statistics.",
    "requirements": "Requirements: NO venv, no GPU, no administrator rights, and the measurement itself never touches the network. The one exception is the optional Share Report tab, which performs no computation: publishing opens a temporary Cloudflare quick tunnel, and the first use downloads Cloudflare's cloudflared.exe (about 52 MB) after you confirm it. It runs in Dragonfly's own process on the numpy, scipy and scikit-image that Dragonfly already ships. trimesh is also needed: Dragonfly 2026.1 and 2027.1 bundle it, Dragonfly 2025.1 does not, so a copy (MIT, pure Python) ships INSIDE this plugin and is used only where Dragonfly has none. Every version therefore uses the same library, and still nothing is downloaded. The input must be a Mesh PUBLISHED in the session (visible in the data properties tree). A vessel of about 1200 triangles takes roughly 20 SECONDS on the automatic settings; the measurement runs on a worker thread that streams its stages, and Cancel takes effect at the next stage boundary. EVERY number is in the mesh's own unit and NOTHING is pre-scaled anywhere: Dragonfly stores world coordinates in metres, the vertices are scaled ONCE when they are read (by default into Dragonfly's own display unit for lengths), and from then on lengths are in that unit, areas in unit^2, volumes in unit^3, curvature and torsion in 1/unit. A mesh that is NOT watertight has NO volume, and the report says \"not defined\" rather than printing a number."
  }
}

{
  "title_en": "Vessel Morphometry",
  "title_cn": "Vessel Morphometry（血管形态测量）",
  "menu_en": "Vessel morphometry along the centerline...",
  "version": "1.0",
  "date": "2026-08-08",
  "en": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Measures the lumen of **one vessel**, cross-section by cross-section, along its own centerline, starting from a closed surface **Mesh** published in the session."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The plugin voxelises the mesh interior, skeletonises it, takes the longest geodesic path through the skeleton as the centerline, and then at every sample measures the lumen **two independent ways** plus the differential geometry of the curve (curvature and torsion). It reports the distribution of each quantity along the vessel, not a single average."
        },
        {
          "kind": "note",
          "text": "**Nothing is created or modified.** The plugin reads a published Mesh and writes only the files you explicitly export."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Six steps: **Inputs / Calibration & Preprocessing / Parameters / Preview & QC / Run / Results & Export**, plus an unnumbered **Share Report** tab. Preview and Run publish nothing; every export is an explicit action in step 6."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The two radius definitions, and why both exist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Every result carries **two radii as separate rows**, never one row called \"the radius\":"
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**Inscribed sphere radius** — the largest sphere that fits inside the lumen at that point. This is what the centerline \"Radius\" column of vmtk / 3D Slicer is, and it is bounded by the **SHORT axis** of the cross-section."
        },
        {
          "kind": "bullet",
          "text": "**Equivalent radius sqrt(A/pi)** — the radius of a circle with the same cross-sectional **area**."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The two agree only for a round section. They diverge as the lumen flattens, and the divergence is not small: the reference pipeline's own \"radius\" is about **54% of its sqrt(A/pi)**, a factor of about **1.9**, which is what a lumen flattened around 3.5:1 does to these definitions. Reporting one of them as \"the radius\" is exactly what made the historical numbers impossible to reconcile."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "So the plugin prints both **and the ratio between them**. Read that ratio for your own specimen instead of carrying a figure over from elsewhere: on the two vessels this plugin was built for it measured **1.15** and **1.09** — those lumens are close to round, and quoting 1.9 for them would have been wrong."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The corresponding **diameters** (twice each radius) are reported as their own rows too, because that is the form most existing tables are written in."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Units: the mesh's own, with no manual pre-scaling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "**Never pre-scale the model.** The workflow this plugin replaces scaled the mesh **10x by hand** before measuring and then converted back inconsistently. The result was that lengths came out **10x** too large, areas **100x** too large, and the volume off by a **further** factor of 10 — three different errors from one \"convenience\" step, and all three looked plausible."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Here the conversion happens **exactly once**, and the plugin says what it did. Dragonfly stores world coordinates in **metres**; the vertices are scaled once when they are read into a **working unit** (by default Dragonfly's own display unit for lengths, so the numbers match the GUI's ruler), and from then on every length is in that unit, areas in unit^2, volumes in unit^3, and curvature and torsion in 1/unit."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The working unit is shown in step 2 together with the bounding box in that unit, so the scale can be sanity-checked before anything is measured, and it is recorded in every export. If Dragonfly's length unit is one this plugin cannot label (inches, for example) it falls back to millimetres **and says so** rather than measuring in an unlabelled unit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Torsion is experimental, and has no maximum",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "warn",
          "text": "**Torsion is reported as EXPERIMENTAL, and no torsion MAXIMUM is reported at all.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Torsion needs the **third** derivative of the centerline, and its denominator vanishes wherever the curve is locally straight, so it is undefined at every inflection. Isolated samples therefore dominate any extremum. Measured on one specimen across four voxel sizes, the torsion **maximum** read **54.9 / 17.6 / 15.3 / 33.4 1/mm** while the **mean** stayed within 8% and curvature within 0.4%. A number that moves by 3x with a setting the user never chose is not a measurement, and printing it beside reproducible figures implies that it is one."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "So the mean is reported, labelled experimental, with the **fitting window that produced it** stated; the minimum and maximum print \"not reported\" together with the reason. Samples whose curvature is below a floor are excluded from the torsion statistics and **counted** in the report."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "**Curvature** does not have this problem: it is reproducible across voxel sizes (measured 0.1812 / 0.1708 / 0.1702 / 0.1708 1/mm on one specimen at four voxel sizes) and is reported with a minimum and maximum like any other quantity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "A mesh that is not watertight has no volume",
      "blocks": [
